)     # normal above the horizon\\n    print(f\\\"plate normal elevation .. {elev:.1f} deg above horizon \\\"\\n          f\\\"(orientation={args.plate_orientation})\\\")\\n    if elev > 60.0:'''\\nassert old in s\\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\\n\\nold = '''    print(f\\\"plate tilt from vertical . {math.degrees(math.acos(min(1.0, nz))):.1f} deg\\\"\\n          f\\\" ({args.plate_orientation})\\\")'''\\nnew = '''    print(f\\\"plate normal elevation .. {math.degrees(math.asin(min(1.0, nz))):.1f}\\\"\\n          f\\\" deg above horizon ({args.plate_orientation})\\\")'''\\nassert old in s\\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\\n\\nopen(p, \\\"w\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").write(s)\\nprint(\\\"facing patched\\\")\\nPYEOF\\npython3 /tmp/patch_facing.py && .venv/bin/python scripts/visualize_target.py --test\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_0scL6Zl7sLh4wCW6hV512410", "ui": {"group": null, "chip": "name", "summary": "", "preview": "command", "header": "", "form": "block", "body": "text", "collapse": "never", "highlight": "", "chrome": "", "mutates": true, "undo": false}, "path_arg": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1792653540.6295967, "id": "94ec983d3e06", "tool_call_id": "call_00_0scL6Zl7sLh4wCW6hV512410", "content": "ERROR: command failed (exit 1)\nstdout:\nfacing patched\ntarget: 4 armors x 40 steps, body at [3.  0.  0.5]\ncamera: 1280x1024 fx=800.0 fy=800.0 pos=[0.  0.  0.5] yaw=0.0 deg\nplate normal elevation .. 15.0 deg above horizon (orientation=vertical)\n  A0: depth 3.30 m  bbox  29.2 x  14.1 px  cos -0.97\n  A1: depth 3.00 m  bbox   7.3 x  16.0 px  cos -0.09\n  A2: depth 2.70 m  bbox  35.7 x  17.1 px  cos +0.96\n  A3: depth 3.00 m  bbox   7.3 x  16.0 px  cos -0.09\n\nstderr:\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/scripts/visualize_target.py\", line 512, in <module>\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n                     ^^^^^^\n  File \"/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/scripts/visualize_target.py\", line 491, in main\n    run_self_test(scene, cam, cam_pos, args)\n  File \"/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/scripts/visualize_target.py\", line 410, in run_self_test\n    assert v_high < v_low\n           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nAssertionError\n", "is_error": true, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1792653551.2205522, "id": "d6f23fcd9512", "content": "v 轴符号写反了（对 down-positive 相机 y 又减了一次），修正：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_VgtJFuyymGef2HiyBSQ60153", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/patch_v.py <<'PYEOF'\\np = \\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\"\\ns = open(p, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").read()\\nold = \\\"\\\"\\\"        u = self.fx * pts_cam[..., 0] / z_safe + self.cx\\n        v = self.cy - self.fy * pts_cam[..., 1] / z_safe\\n        return u, v, z\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew = \\\"\\\"\\\"        u = self.fx * pts_cam[..., 0] / z_safe + self.cx\\n        v = self.cy + self.fy * pts_cam[..., 1] / z_safe     # +Y is down\\n        return u, v, z\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nassert old in s\\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\\nopen(p, \\\"w\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").write(s)\\nprint(\\\"v fixed\\\")\\nPYEOF\\npython3 /tmp/patch_v.py && .venv/bin/python scripts/visualize_target.py --test\"}"}], "reasoning": "Great progress: the projections now look sane (A2 nearest, 35.7×17.1 px, front-facing; A1/A3 edge-on 7.3 px wide ✓).\n\nNow `v_high < v_low` failed. v_low for p=(3,0,0.10) and v_high for p=(3,0,0.60), camera at z=0.5. cam y = -(z_world - 0.5) = -(0.1-0.5)= +0.4 → v = cy - fy*0.4/3 = cy - 106 (above center) — wait v = cy - fy*y_cam/z = cy - 800*0.4/3 = cy - 107.\n\nFor z_world=0.6: y_cam = -0.1 → v = cy + 26.7.\n\nSo v_high (0.6) = cy+27 > v_low (0.1) = cy-107. My assertion is backwards! A higher world point should have a LARGER v (lower in image)? No wait — in image coordinates, v down; a higher world point appears higher in the image → smaller v. Let me recompute: world z=0.6 is above camera z=0.5 → should appear above the center → v < cy. But I computed y_cam = -(z - 0.5) = -(0.6-0.5) = -0.1 → v = cy - 800*(-0.1)/3 = cy + 26.7 → BELOW center. That's wrong!\n\nSo the y sign is inverted! Let me check: camera Y axis should point DOWN in the world = -Z. So y_cam = -(z_world) → for a point above the camera, y_cam < 0 → v = cy - fy*y_cam/z... Hold on: standard pinhole: v = cy - fy * y_cam / z_cam where y_cam is down-positive. If y_cam is down-positive and the point is above (y_cam negative), then v = cy - fy*(negative)/z = cy + positive → larger v → below center. That's the contradiction: the minus sign in my formula is only correct if y_cam is UP-positive.\n\nLet me redo carefully. Convention: camera frame +Y down. Projection: v = cy + fy * (y_cam / z_cam) (no minus), because y_cam positive = down = larger v. And u = cx + fx * (x_cam / z_cam) with +X right.\n\nBut my R_wc base maps world up (+Z) → camera Y = -Z = down ✓ (base row1 = (0,0,-1)). So y_cam is down-positive. Therefore v = cy + fy*y_cam/z. I wrote `v = cy - fy*y_cam/z` — that's the UP-positive formula, i.e., I double-flipped. That's a real bug in my new camera model: vertical positions are mirrored.\n\nThe original script had v = fy*y_cam/z with y_cam down-positive and then flipped the axis via set_ylim(800, -800) — i.e., original: v_pixel = +y_cam (down positive) then inverted axis → wrong for a normal image display? Let's check: original: v = fy*y/z with y down-positive; then `set_ylim(800,-800)` makes larger v appear at the top of the plot. So the original ALSO double-flipped → the original image was vertically mirrored! Hmm, wait: set_ylim(bottom=800, top=-800) means v=800 at the bottom... no: set_ylim(lo, hi) with lo=800, hi=-800 → the axis is inverted: value 800 at the bottom? In matplotlib, set_ylim(bottom, top). bottom=800 → the bottom of the axes corresponds to 800, top=-800. So a point with larger v is drawn... at larger v = 800 = bottom. Hmm no: bottom=800 means the y-value at the bottom edge is 800 → so larger v → lower. So the original set_ylim(800,-800) does NOT invert; it means top=-800? Let's think again: set_ylim(800, -800) sets ymin=800, ymax=-800, which is an inverted axis (ymin > ymax) → the axis runs from 800 at the bottom to -800 at the top → so v=800 maps to the bottom, v=-800 to the top → larger v is LOWER. That's the normal image convention (v down). So the original was: v = fy*y_cam/z with y down → larger v = lower in the plot = correct image orientation ✓. So the original was correct, and my \"cy - fy*y/z\" is a mirror bug.\n\nBut wait, then the original's comment \"图像 y 向下\" with ylim(800,-800)... yes consistent: v is down-positive and the axis is inverted so down-positive renders downward. Hmm, actually set_ylim(800, -800) inverts the axis: bottom=800 (down-positive values at bottom) ✓ correct.\n\nOK so fix my formula: `v = self.cy + self.fy * pts_cam[..., 1] / z_safe`.\n\nThen v_low (z=0.1, below camera): y_cam = -(0.1-0.5) = +0.4 → v = cy + 107 (lower) ✓; v_high (0.6): y_cam=-0.1 → v = cy - 27 (higher) ✓. And the assertion v_high < v_low holds ✓.\n\nAlso earlier the geometry check `up -> [0,-1,0]` remains valid.\n\nBut note: this changes the rendering of armors' vertical positions — with the fix, armors at z=0.55 appear above center ✓ correct.\n\nLet me patch the formula and rerun. Also make sure the earlier test \"armor centers inside frame\" still passes (v within 0..H: offsets ±0.05 → ±13 px, fine).\n\nAlso, is the horizontal mirror right? u = cx + fx*x_cam/z with x_cam right ✓, and we saw A1 (world +Y = left) → u < cx ✓.\n\nPatch."}
{"type": "tool_call", "timestamp": 1792653551.2204943, "id": "06cde8a9d5f9", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/patch_v.py <<'PYEOF'\\np = \\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\"\\ns = open(p, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").read()\\nold = \\\"\\\"\\\"        u = self.fx * pts_cam[..., 0] / z_safe + self.cx\\n        v = self.cy - self.fy * pts_cam[..., 1] / z_safe\\n        return u, v, z\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew = \\\"\\\"\\\"        u = self.fx * pts_cam[..., 0] / z_safe + self.cx\\n        v = self.cy + self.fy * pts_cam[..., 1] / z_safe     # +Y is down\\n        return u, v, z\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nassert old in s\\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\\nopen(p, \\\"w\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").write(s)\\nprint(\\\"v fixed\\\")\\nPYEOF\\npython3 /tmp/patch_v.py && .venv/bin/python scripts/visualize_target.py --test\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_VgtJFuyymGef2HiyBSQ60153", "ui": {"group": null, "chip": "name", "summary": "", "preview": "command", "header": "", "form": "block", "body": "text", "collapse": "never", "highlight": "", "chrome": "", "mutates": true, "undo": false}, "path_arg": ""}
