{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1792653651.847213, "id": "8c196e9a1a7d", "content": "`armor_yaws` 在竖直模式下会偏 90°（它取的是板的宽度轴）。改成用法向计算，legacy 数值不变：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_WBDsMXKWr9cayayXXTUT2007", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"grep -rn \\\"armor_yaws\\\" --include=*.py . | grep -v \\\".venv\\\"\"}"}], "reasoning": "The vertical orientation is exactly as intended:\n- pitch 0: normal (1,0,0) radial, extent x=0 (thin plate), y=0.12 (width tangential), z=0.06 (height vertical) ✓\n- pitch 15: normal (0.966,0,0.259) → 15° up, z extent 0.058, x 0.016 ✓\n- horizontal legacy: normal (0,0,1) → flat ✓, extent 0.12 × 0.06 × 0 ✓\n\n`armor_yaws` for vertical: [1.571, -3.142, -1.571, 0] for plates at θ=0,90,180,270. Hmm: plate 0 at θ=0 has normal +X → yaw should be 0, but `armor_yaws[0]` = 1.571. Let me check: armor_yaws = atan2(world_rot[1,0], world_rot[0,0]).\n\nFor vertical, R = Rz(θ) Ry(-p) R_up. Column 0 of R = Rz(θ)Ry(-p) R_up e_x = Rz(θ) Ry(-p) (0,1,0) = Rz(θ)(0,1,0) = (-sinθ, cosθ, 0). So R[0,0] = -sinθ, R[1,0] = cosθ → atan2(cosθ, -sinθ) = 90° + θ. So armor_yaws = θ + 90°. For θ=0 → 90° = 1.571 ✓ matches.\n\nSo the reported armor_yaws are offset by +90° relative to the plate's outward normal direction (the normal is +X at θ=0 but yaw says 90°). Hmm — which is \"correct\"? The convention for armor_yaw in RoboMaster usually means the yaw of the plate's facing normal, so at θ=0 (plate at +X, normal +X) yaw should be 0.\n\nIn the horizontal (legacy) case: column 0 = Rz(θ)Ry(p) e_x = Rz(θ)(cos p, 0, -sin p) = (cosθ cos p, sinθ cos p, -sin p) → atan2(sinθ cos p, cosθ cos p) = θ ✓ so yaw = θ, but there the \"normal\" is +Z (up), so the \"yaw\" is tangent to the plate... i.e., in the legacy case, yaw = θ is the direction of the plate's width axis, which is radial = the outward radial direction. So armor_yaws has always meant \"direction of the width axis\" = radial direction (θ).\n\nIn my vertical case, the width axis is tangential and the normal is radial, so `armor_yaws` = θ+90 = the tangential direction (the width axis). Consistent with the legacy definition (\"column 0 of the rotation\" = yaw of the local X axis = width direction), but if downstream code interprets armor_yaws as the plate's facing direction, the vertical orientation would be off by 90°.\n\nHmm. What does armor_yaws mean semantically? Given the plates face radially outward and the legacy width axis is radial, in the legacy model width direction == facing direction, so the two interpretations coincide. With vertical plates they don't.\n\nWhich interpretation is intended downstream? Nothing else in the repo consumes armor_yaws yet (grep). But for correctness I should make the vertical case report the *normal* yaw = θ, since a user computing \"armor yaw\" almost certainly wants the facing direction.\n\nThe cleanest: compute `armor_yaws` from the plate normal projected onto the XY plane:\n`armor_yaws = atan2(n_y, n_x)` with n = world_rot @ [0,0,1] (local Z = normal).\n\nFor legacy: normal = Rz(θ)Ry(p) e_z = (cosθ sin p, sinθ sin p, cos p) → atan2 = θ ✓ (same as before).\nFor vertical: normal = (cosθ cos p, sinθ cos p, sin p) → atan2 = θ ✓.\n\nSo computing from the normal gives θ in both cases — but for the legacy default pitch=15 this is also θ (same value as before), so it's backward compatible for legacy.\n\nLet me change the implementation to compute armor_yaws from the normal. That's more meaningful and consistent.\n\nImplementation:\n```python\n        normals = torch.einsum(\"btmij,j->btmi\", world_rot, torch.tensor([0.0, 0.0, 1.0], device=device, dtype=radius.dtype))\n        armor_yaws = torch.atan2(normals[..., 1], normals[..., 0])\n```\nThat's the facing yaw regardless of orientation. For legacy the value is identical to before (θ). Good, no behavior change for legacy.\n\nLet me verify no other code depends on armor_yaws: grep.\n\nThen re-run env check to see yaws = [0, 1.571, ±3.14, -1.571] for both orientations.\n\nThen regenerate the PNG and finish.\n\nAlso the script doesn't use armor_yaws; fine.\n\nLet me patch."}
