{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793174623.801676, "id": "27160f6d790b", "tool_call_id": "call_00_dccpawFg52DS1B4KAznU9095", "content": "import math\n\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\n\nfrom diff_env.geometry import euler_to_rotmat\n\n\nclass BodyMotion(nn.Module):\n    def forward(self, t: torch.Tensor, params: dict):\n        bsz, sln = t.shape\n        device = t.device\n\n        def _get(key: str, shape: tuple, default: float = 0.0):\n            val = params.get(key, None)\n            if val is None:\n                return torch.full(shape, default, device=device, dtype=t.dtype)\n            if isinstance(val, torch.Tensor):\n                return val.to(device=device, dtype=t.dtype)\n            return torch.full(shape, float(val), device=device, dtype=t.dtype)\n\n        t_e = t.unsqueeze(-1)\n\n        p0 = _get(\"p0\", (bsz, 3))\n        v = _get(\"v\", (bsz, 3))\n        a = _get(\"a\", (bsz, 3))\n        sinus_amp = _get(\"sinus_amp\", (bsz, 3))\n        sinus_freq = _get(\"sinus_freq\", (bsz, 3))\n        rw_sigma = _get(\"rw_sigma\", (bsz, 3))\n\n        pos = p0.unsqueeze(1) + v.unsqueeze(1) * t_e + 0.5 * a.unsqueeze(1) * t_e**2\n\n        if sinus_amp.abs().sum() > 0:\n            pos = pos + sinus_amp.unsqueeze(1) * torch.sin(\n                2 * math.pi * sinus_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t_e\n            )\n\n        if rw_sigma.abs().sum() > 0:\n            increments = torch.randn(\n                bsz, sln, 3, device=device, dtype=t.dtype\n            ) * rw_sigma.unsqueeze(1)\n            pos = pos + torch.cumsum(increments, dim=1)\n\n        yaw_0 = _get(\"yaw_0\", (bsz,))\n        yaw_rate = _get(\"yaw_rate\", (bsz,))\n        yaw_amp = _get(\"yaw_amp\", (bsz,))\n        yaw_freq = _get(\"yaw_freq\", (bsz,))\n\n        pitch_0 = _get(\"pitch_0\", (bsz,))\n        pitch_amp = _get(\"pitch_amp\", (bsz,))\n        pitch_freq = _get(\"pitch_freq\", (bsz,))\n\n        roll_0 = _get(\"roll_0\", (bsz,))\n        roll_amp = _get(\"roll_amp\", (bsz,))\n        roll_freq = _get(\"roll_freq\", (bsz,))\n\n        yaw = yaw_0.unsqueeze(1) + yaw_rate.unsqueeze(1) * t\n        if yaw_amp.abs().sum() > 0:\n            yaw = yaw + yaw_amp.unsqueeze(1) * torch.sin(\n                2 * math.pi * yaw_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t\n            )\n\n        pitch = pitch_0.unsqueeze(1).expand(-1, sln)\n        if pitch_amp.abs().sum() > 0:\n            pitch = pitch + pitch_amp.unsqueeze(1) * torch.sin(\n                2 * math.pi * pitch_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t\n            )\n\n        roll = roll_0.unsqueeze(1).expand(-1, sln)\n        if roll_amp.abs().sum() > 0:\n            roll = roll + roll_amp.unsqueeze(1) * torch.sin(\n                2 * math.pi * roll_freq.unsqueeze(1) * t\n            )\n\n        rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaw, pitch, roll)\n\n        return {\"positions\": pos, \"rotations\": rotations}\n\n\nclass ArmorLayout(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, armor_width: float = 0.12, armor_height: float = 0.06):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.armor_width = armor_width\n        self.armor_height = armor_height\n\n    def forward(\n        self,\n        n_armors: int,\n        radius: torch.Tensor,\n        radius_offsets: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n        height_offsets: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n        roll: float = math.pi * 5 / 12,\n    ):\n        device = radius.device\n        bsz = radius.shape[0]\n\n        if radius_offsets is None:\n            radius_offsets = torch.zeros(\n                bsz, n_armors, device=device, dtype=radius.dtype\n            )\n        if height_offsets is None:\n            height_offsets = torch.zeros(\n                bsz, n_armors, device=device, dtype=radius.dtype\n            )\n\n        theta = (\n            torch.arange(n_armors, device=device, dtype=radius.dtype)\n            * 2\n            * math.pi\n            / n_armors\n        )\n\n        r_eff = radius.unsqueeze(-1) + radius_offsets\n        local_x = r_eff * torch.cos(theta)\n        local_y = r_eff * torch.sin(theta)\n        local_z = height_offsets\n        local_positions = torch.stack([local_x, local_y, local_z], dim=-1)\n\n        yaws = theta.unsqueeze(0).expand(bsz, -1)\n        # vertical armor: width tangential, height up, normal radial outward\n        zeros = torch.zeros_like(yaws)\n        rolls = torch.full_like(yaws, roll)\n        half_pi = torch.full_like(yaws, math.pi / 2)\n        local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws + half_pi, zeros, rolls)\n\n        w2 = self.armor_width / 2\n        h2 = self.armor_height / 2\n        corners_local = torch.tensor(\n            [\n                [-w2, -h2, 0.0],\n                [w2, -h2, 0.0],\n                [w2, h2, 0.0],\n                [-w2, h2, 0.0],\n            ],\n            device=device,\n            dtype=radius.dtype,\n        )\n\n        return local_positions, local_rotations, corners_local\n\n\nclass TargetMotion(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, armor_width: float = 0.12, armor_height: float = 0.06):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.body_motion = BodyMotion()\n        self.armor_layout = ArmorLayout(armor_width, armor_height)\n\n    def forward(self, t, mode=\"static\", **params):\n        bsz, _ = t.shape\n        device = t.device\n\n        if mode in (\"static\", \"constant_velocity\"):\n            n_armors = 1\n        else:\n            n_armors = params.get(\"n_armors\", 4)\n\n        radius = params.get(\"radius\", None)\n        if radius is None:\n            radius = torch.full((bsz,), 0.2, device=device, dtype=t.dtype)\n        elif not isinstance(radius, torch.Tensor):\n            radius = torch.full((bsz,), float(radius), device=device, dtype=t.dtype)\n\n        radius_offsets = params.get(\"armor_radius_offsets\", None)\n        height_offsets = params.get(\"armor_height_offsets\", None)\n\n        body = self.body_motion(t, params)\n        body_pos = body[\"positions\"]\n        body_rot = body[\"rotations\"]\n\n        local_pos, local_rot, corners_local = self.armor_layout(\n            n_armors, radius, radius_offsets, height_offsets\n        )\n\n        centers = body_pos.unsqueeze(2) + torch.einsum(\n            \"btij,bmj->btmi\", body_rot, local_pos\n        )\n        world_rot = torch.einsum(\"btik,bmkj->btmij\", body_rot, local_rot)\n        corners = centers.unsqueeze(-2) + torch.einsum(\n            \"btmij,cj->btmci\", world_rot, corners_local\n        )\n        # facing direction of each plate: local +Z is the plate normal\n        normal_local = torch.tensor([0.0, 0.0, 1.0], device=device, dtype=radius.dtype)\n        world_normal = torch.einsum(\"btmij,j->btmi\", world_rot, normal_local)\n        armor_yaws = torch.atan2(world_normal[..., 1], world_normal[..., 0])\n\n        return {\n            \"corners\": corners,\n            \"centers\": centers,\n            \"armor_yaws\": armor_yaws,\n            \"armor_rotations\": world_rot,\n            \"body_rotations\": body_rot,\n        }\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793174632.252513, "id": "f186dca20d53", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_5fm538AsHlTLwtQMIGnR0363", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && git diff -- scripts/visualize_target.py | head -200\"}"}, {"id": "call_01_wp7cIDS1eys2vZELtakd7134", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && git diff -- fix-vis.clc | head -60\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now note: `ArmorLayout.forward(... roll: float = math.pi * 5 / 12)` — default roll is 75° now?? Earlier it was roll=π/2 with `local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws + half_pi, zeros, roll)`. Hmm, my sed changed `euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, half_pi)` → `euler_to_rotmat(yaws + half_pi, zeros, half_pi)`, but the current file says `rolls = torch.full_like(yaws, roll)` and `half_pi` — so somebody later changed to parameterized roll (default 75°). My sed applied if the pattern existed; it doesn't now, so my sed was a no-op? Actually my sed ran successfully (no error), but if pattern absent, sed succeeds without changes. The file mtime would still update. So my earlier \"fix\" did nothing. Good—no damage. Actually wait: the summary read showed `local_rotations = euler_to_rotmat(yaws, zeros, half_pi)` at line 122 and my sed output showed the file with `yaws + half_pi`. Then later re-read now shows `rolls`/`roll` parameter with default π*5/12. Hmm, that means the file changed between my sed and now? mtime: diff_env/target.py 10月 22 16:02 earlier... The user has since edited. Anyway current state: `euler_to_rotmat(yaws + half_pi, zeros, rolls)` with roll=75° default. So the armor is tilted (not vertical) — roll 75° gives a tilted plate. That's a \"tilted armor\" thing. Also note the default changed from π/2 to 5π/12 which the animation shows as tilted plates.\n\nNow the key question: animation speed mismatch.\n\nIn `update`, an important detail: `out[\"centers\"][0, index]` — shape (b, t, m, 3) → [0, index] gives (m,3) fine.\n\nNow speed: The animation uses `interval=1000/fps`. Matplotlib's FuncAnimation with interval=100 will try to redraw every 100ms. But redraw cost of two big axes with clear() is high. With blit=False and ax.clear() each frame, each frame draw may take 150-400 ms → effective fps ~3, so the whole 10s animation takes ~30s and appears slow. That's the classic cause.\n\nBut is there another bug making speed *faster*? Look: `n_frames = int(duration * fps)` = 100; `torch.linspace(0, duration, 100)` → step 0.10101 s. Fine ~1:1.\n\nHmm, what about `TargetMotion` time: yaw = yaw_0 + yaw_rate * t, yaw_rate=3 rad/s? BodyMotion takes yaw in radians (converted via euler_to_rotmat). yaw_rate=3 passed raw → 3 rad/s ≈ 172°/s. That's fast! And unknown to the user expecting e.g. 3 deg/s or 3 rad/... Actually is yaw_rate intended in rad/s? euler_to_rotmat takes radians. So yaw_rate=3 rad/s → full rotation in 2.1 s. That may look \"faster than expected\" if the user thought 3 = deg/s or 3 rad/... Hmm.\n\nBut the question says \"animation speed\" (动画速度) — playback speed, not target rotation.\n\nLet me think about `plt.show()` + FuncAnimation in a script run under Agg? No `matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")` now (removed). So interactive backend needed. In a TTY-free environment, plt.show() would... The user runs it presumably with a display, maybe in VS Code.\n\nCritical bug #1: `ax` is undefined (commented out) → NameError at first frame. So the script crashes. But then the user wouldn't see an animation... unless the update is never called? With `plt.show()` and Agg backend, show() returns immediately (non-blocking, warning \"FigureCanvasAgg is non-interactive\"), so update never runs, no error, and no animation. Hmm! That matches: if the user's backend is Agg (no display / headless), `plt.show()` just warns and exits — no animation window at all. But the user says they see an animation with wrong speed...\n\nHold on. Let'