{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793174856.6579764, "id": "29a23764b749", "tool_call_id": "call_00_70DC2gMNUBJoDM6bx4UC2026", "content": "60: \n61: def update(index: int, out: dict, args, fig, ax, ax2, ax_img):\n62:     centers = out[\"centers\"][0, index].numpy()\n63:     corners = out[\"corners\"][0, index].numpy()          # (M, 4, 3)\n64:     body_rot = out[\"body_rotations\"][0, index].numpy()\n65:     armor_rot = out[\"armor_rotations\"][0, index].numpy()\n66:     \n67:     ax.clear()\n68:     ax2.clear()\n69:     ax_img.clear()\n70: \n71:     w, h = args.image_size\n72:     cam_pos = np.array([0.0, 0.0, args.height])\n73:     body = np.array([args.distance, 0.0, args.height])\n74:     R_wc = camera_rotation(args.cam_yaw)\n75:     u, v, depth = project(corners, R_wc, cam_pos, args.fx, args.fx, w / 2, h / 2)\n76:     \n77:     \n78:     # a plate faces the camera when its normal points towards it\n79:     normal = np.cross(corners[:, 1] - corners[:, 0], corners[:, 3] - corners[:, 0])\n... (showing lines 60-79 of 179; use offset=80 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793174856.6823068, "id": "6999b61ae6c7", "tool_call_id": "call_01_AGjDI1VBgrN6OEiMmRRV0401", "content": "136:     p.add_argument(\"--image-size\", type=int, nargs=2, default=[1280, 1024],\n137:                    metavar=(\"W\", \"H\"))\n138:     p.add_argument(\"--dpi\", type=int, default=130)\n139:     p.add_argument(\"--duration\", type=float, default=10.0)\n140:     p.add_argument(\"--fps\", type=int, default=10.0)\n141:     args = p.parse_args(argv)\n142:     \n143:     n_frames = int(args.duration * args.fps)\n144:     t = torch.linspace(0.0, args.duration, n_frames).unsqueeze(0)\n145: \n146:     # one snapshot of the target\n147:     out = TargetMotion()(\n148:         t,\n149:         \"complex\",\n150:         p0=torch.tensor([[args.distance, 0.0, args.height]]),\n151:         yaw_0=torch.tensor([math.radians(args.yaw)]),\n152:         yaw_rate=torch.tensor([10]),\n153:         n_armors=4,\n154:         radius=torch.tensor([args.radius]),\n155:     )\n156:     \n157:     fig = plt.figure(figsize=(16.5, 5.2))\n158:     ax     = fig.add_subplot(1, 3, 1, projection=\"3d\")\n159:     ax2    = fig.add_subplot(1, 3, 2, projection=\"3d\")\n160:     ax_img = fig.add_subplot(1, 3, 3)\n161:     \n162:     fig.subplots_adjust(left=0.04, right=0.98, bottom=0.10, top=0.85, wspace=0.15)\n163:     \n164:     from matplotlib.animation import FuncAnimation\n165:     ani = FuncAnimation(\n166:         fig, \n167:         lambda i: update(i, out, args, fig, ax, ax2, ax_img),\n168:         frames=n_frames,\n169:         interval=1000.0 / args.fps,\n170:         blit=False,\n171:         repeat=False\n172:     )\n173:     \n174:     plt.show()\n175:     return 0\n176: \n177: \n... (showing lines 136-177 of 179; use offset=178 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793174887.8088222, "id": "df739e21cd92", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_GPHFkY8Gg0aahQMhmxuE6110", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\": 18, \"offset\": 1, \"path\": \"scripts/visualize_target.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Note: the file has changed since my last read — line 158 now HAS `ax = fig.add_subplot(...)` (uncommented!) and line 152 now `yaw_rate=torch.tensor([10])` instead of 3. Total lines 179 now. So the user edited between my read and now (they were probably editing live). So the `ax` bug is gone and yaw_rate is 10 rad/s (573°/s, 1.6 rev/s).\n\nSo now the file: ax defined, yaw_rate=10. The user asks specifically: \"说一下实际时间跳帧的修法，直说修改和添加哪些地方\" — describe the wall-clock frame-skip fix, be direct about what to modify and add where.\n\nI should give a precise, minimal patch spec against the current line numbers:\n\n1. Add `import time` at top (line ~9-11 area, after `import math`).\n2. In `main`, after `args = p.parse_args(argv)` / before FuncAnimation, add `clock = {\"t0\": time.perf_counter()}` or use a closure start time.\n3. Replace the lambda with a named `def on_frame(_i)` that computes index from wall clock.\n4. Add a small helper `frame_index` or inline.\n5. Ensure `frames` can be None? With `FuncAnimation`, if frames is None and update returns artists... Using `frames=None` means infinite via `itertools.count`? In matplotlib, `frames=None` → `itertools.count()`? Actually: if frames is None, `self._iter_gen = itertools.count`?? Let me recall matplotlib source:\n\n```python\nif frames is None:\n    self._iter_gen = itertools.count\nelif callable(frames):\n    self._iter_gen = frames\nelif iterable(frames):\n    self._iter_gen = lambda: iter(frames)\n    ...\n```\nHmm in newer versions:\n```python\n        if frames is None:\n            self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n        elif callable(frames):\n            self._iter_gen = frames\n        else:\n            self._iter_gen = lambda: iter(frames)\n            ...\n            self._framedata = frames  # cached\n```\nWith `frames=None`, it iterates indefinitely (0,1,2,...) — good for a real-time loop that must run until duration elapses. But then we need to stop ourselves when the sim time exceeds duration: call `ani.event_source.stop()` (or `ani.pause()`? `event_source.stop()`) and/or `plt.close(fig)`.\n\nImportant detail: `FuncAnimation` passes the frame value; with `frames=None` we'd get increasing ints but we ignore them and compute our own index from the wall clock.\n\nAlso note: if `frames=None`, matplotlib 3.11 may set `self._save_count = None`; saving isn't used here.\n\nAlternative simpler approach without FuncAnimation: use `fig.canvas.new_timer`? But keep FuncAnimation for minimal diff. Actually the minimal, most robust approach: keep `FuncAnimation` but pass `frames=None` (or `itertools.count()`), and in the callback compute index from `time.perf_counter() - t0`; when index > n_frames-1, clamp and stop.\n\nAlso drift: don't accumulate dt in the loop; always use absolute elapsed time so slow frames self-correct (drop frames). That's the core of \"实际时间跳帧\".\n\nAlso to be safe: `out` tensors index with Python int.\n\nAlso guard: if index unchanged since last update, return early (skip redundant redraw) — optional but helps.\n\nAdditionally `plt.show()` blocking — fine.\n\nAlso mention: if you want a fixed wall time independent of `--duration` scaling, add `--speed` or use `--duration` as the sim length.\n\nLet me write the exact patch with current line numbers:\n\n**改动 1 — 顶部加 import（第 9–11 行区域）**\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport math\nimport time\n```\nLine 9 `import argparse`, line 10 `import math`, so add line 11 `import time`.\n\n**改动 2 — 在 `main` 里，第 144 行后（`t = torch.linspace...` 之后）加两个量**\n\nActually better place: after `n_frames`/`t` computation (line 143-144), add:\n```python\n    dt = args.duration / (n_frames - 1)     # 与 linspace 一致的帧间隔\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()                # 挂钟起点\n```\nBut `t0` must be set when animation actually starts, not when main builds the figure (a few hundred ms of construction). Better: start clock at first callback — use a mutable dict or `nonlocal`. Simplest: initialize `t0 = [None]` and in callback set on first call. Or set `t0` right before `plt.show()`? Actually the first frame happens after show() starts the timer, so `t0 = time.perf_counter()` placed just before `plt.show()` is accurate within one frame. Hmm, but construction is before that; place `t0 = time.perf_counter()` immediately before `plt.show()` (line 173) — but the callback closure needs it. Define closure after t0.\n\nCleanest minimal structure: replace lines 164–174 with:\n\n```python\n    from matplotlib.animation import FuncAnimation\n    import itertools\n\n    def on_frame(_frame):                        # 帧号由挂钟决定，忽略 matplotlib 给的序号\n        i = int((time.perf_counter() - clock[0]) * args.fps)\n        if i >= n_frames:                        # 到点就停\n            i = n_frames - 1\n            update(i, out, args, fig, ax, ax2, ax_img)\n            fig.canvas.draw_idle()\n            ani.event_source.stop()\n            return\n        update(i, out, args, fig, ax, ax2, ax_img)\n\n    clock = [time.perf_counter()]\n    ani = FuncAnimation(\n        fig,\n        on_frame,\n        frames=None,          # 不限帧数，由挂钟/时长决定结束\n        interval=1000.0 / args.fps,\n        blit=False,\n        repeat=False,\n    )\n    plt.show()\n    return 0\n```\n\nWait: `clock` referenced in on_frame before assignment — Python closure over `main` local `clock` defined later at runtime is fine as long as on_frame is called after `clock` assignment. But `clock = [...]` is after def, before FuncAnimation → fine. But careful: `clock` inside `on_frame` is a free variable of main; main assigns it → captured by reference at call time. Fine.\n\nHowever, `frames=None` with `save_count` deprecation: in mpl ≥3.7, `frames=None` + `save_count` default 100 warnings? Let me recall: \n\n```python\n        if frames is None:\n            self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n        ...\n        if self._save_count is None:\n            if self._iter_gen is itertools.count:  # something\n```\nActually matplotlib code (3.8+):\n\n```python\n        if isinstance(frames, numbers.Integral):\n            ...\n        elif frames is None:\n            if self._save_count is None:\n                self._save_count = 100\n                _api.warn_external(...)\n```\nHmm. Let me recall precisely. In matplotlib/animation.py:\n\n```python\n        # If the animation is going to be saved, this is the count\n        ...\n        if frames is None:\n            self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n        elif callable(frames):\n            self._iter_gen = frames\n        elif isinstance(frames, numbers.Integral):\n            # If we're passed in an integer, we'll make it the number of frames\n            self._iter_gen = lambda: range(frames)\n            self._save_count = frames\n            self._cache_frame_data = False\n        else:\n            self._iter_gen = lambda: iter(frames)\n            self._save_count = len(frames) if ...\n```\nAnd earlier:\n```python\n        if save_count is not None:\n            self._save_count = save_count\n        else:\n            self._save_count = None\n```\nThen in `_init_draw`/save path, `_save_count` used by `_get_animation_length`? There is a deprecation: \"The value of the save_count parameter is ignored...\" Hmm.\n\nTo be safe, pass `frames=None` and also `save_count=n_frames` to avoid the \"save_count=100\" default warning. Actually with frames=None, the warning in mpl 3.7+: \n```\nif frames is None:\n    self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n```\nand later in `save`, uses `self._save_count`... The known UserWarning: \"Animation was deleted without rendering anything...\" is different.\n\nI recall in mpl ≥ 3.6, `FuncAnimation.__init__` has:\n```python\n        if frames is None:\n            self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n```\nand there's this in `save`? Not essential. Since we call `plt.show()` and never save, `save_count` irrelevant. But passing `save_count=n_frames` is harmless and silences any warning. I'll include it.\n\nSimpler alternative avoiding `frames=None` subtleties: keep `frames=itertools.count()` — same thing.\n\nEven simpler and fully in our control: skip FuncAnimation entirely and use `fig.canvas.new_timer(interval=...)`:\n\n```python\n    def on_frame():\n        i = int((time.perf_counter() - t0) * args.fps)\n        ...\n        if i >= n_frames:\n            timer.stop()\n            return\n        update(...)\n    timer = fig.canvas.new_timer(interval=1000/args.fps)\n    timer.add_callback(on_frame)\n    timer.start()\n    plt.show()\n```\nThis is arguably cleaner and avoids FuncAnimation's frame bookkeeping entirely — the callback takes no args, so no index juggling. But FuncAnimation keeps a strong ref and handles the draw; with `new_timer` we must keep `timer` alive (local var, show() blocks → fine).\n\nThe user asked specifically \"实际时间跳帧的修法\" — wall-clock frame skipping. I'll present the main patch using FuncAnimation (small diff from existing code), plus mention the `new_timer` variant briefly. Keep it focused: they said \"直说修改和添加哪些地方\" — plainly say which places to modify/add.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n### 需要动的 4 个地方 (per file lines)\n1. `scripts/visualize_target.py:11` — add `import time`.\n2. `scripts/visualize_target.py:143-144` — add `dt` (optional) and `clock` holder.\n3. `scripts/visualize_target.py:164-174` — replace the `FuncAnimation` block: named callback computing index from wall clock, `frames=None`, stop at end. Plus add `interval` correction.\n4. `scripts/visualize_target.py:75` — depth assert (unrelated? skip; not asked). Keep focused; maybe just add the drop-last-frame guard.\n\nAlso important nuance: `n_frames = int(duration*fps)` and index = int(elapsed*fps) with clamping.\n\nAlso mention that the callback ignores `_frame` and that skipped frames are dropped, not accumulated: index comes from absolute elapsed time ⇒ no drift, and after a slow frame it jumps forward (true frame skipping). This is the essential mechanism.\n\nAlso: because rendering is slow, set the timer interval slightly smaller than the target period (e.g. `interval=max(1, 1000/fps/2)`) so that the callback runs often enough to land near the desired index — actually with interval=100ms and render 150ms, the timer will run back-to-back anyway. But if render is fast (2 ms) and fps=10, interval=100 ms yields exactly 10 index steps/s — fine. Setting interval = period/2 gives finer granularity and less quantization jitter (max error ~half frame). I'll mention `interval=min(1000.0/args.fps, 50)` or `0.5*period`. Keep simple: `interval=1000.0 / args.fps / 2`.\n\nPrecision detail: index = int(elapsed*fps) can skip or repeat; when repeated, redraw is wasted. Add early return if index == last[0]. That's a nice touch: `if i == last[\"i\"]: return`.\n\nAlso timeout: `--duration` is the sim length; the playback takes `duration` wall seconds. Good.\n\nAnother important detail: the last frame. When elapsed > duration, we should draw the final frame once and stop. Handle with the clamp + flag.\n\nAlso mention: `plt.show()` returns when window closed; timer stops itself.\n\nLet me also mention how to verify: run with `--duration 10 --fps 10`; time it with `time` command; should be ~10 s wall. And the target spins 10 rad/s ⇒ 15.9 turns in 10 s.\n\nAlso flag that `yaw_rate=10` at line 152 is 573°/s (1.6 rev/s) — with a 10 s clip that's 15.9 turns; if the user expects slower spin, lower yaw_rate, independent of the frame-skip fix. Since they mention speed earlier, brief note.\n\nAlso note line 140 `--fps` type=int but 10.0 default → change to float and use `round()` to avoid `int` truncation; and guard fps>0.\n\nLet me now produce a clear answer with exact old→new blocks. Keep it tight and direct as requested: \"直说修改和添加哪些地方\".\n\nI'll provide:\n\n**A. 第 11 行：加 `import time`**\n\n**B. 第 143–144 行：把帧数计算换成带 dt 的版本** \n```python\n    n_frames = max(2, int(round(args.duration * args.fps)))\n    t = torch.linspace(0.0, args.duration, n_frames).unsqueeze(0)\n    period = 1000.0 / args.fps          # ms，一帧的挂钟时长\n    clock = {\"t0\": None, \"i\": -1}       # 挂钟起点 / 上次绘制的帧号\n```\nHmm, `clock` used in callback; fine.\n\n**C. 第 164–174 行：替换整个 FuncAnimation 块**\n\n```python\n    from matplotlib.animation import FuncAnimation\n\n    def on_frame(_):\n        if clock[\"t0\"] is None:                      # 第一帧才开始计时\n            clock[\"t0\"] = time.perf_counter()\n        i = int((time.perf_counter() - clock[\"t0\"]) / args.duration * n_frames)\n        i = min(i, n_frames - 1)                     # 到点画最后一帧\n        if i == clock[\"i\"]:                          # 挂钟还没走完一格，跳过重画\n            return\n        clock[\"i\"] = i\n        update(i, out, args, fig, ax, ax2, ax_img)\n        if i == n_frames - 1:\n            ani.event_source.stop()\n\n    ani = FuncAnimation(\n        fig,\n        on_frame,\n        frames=None,                 # 不再用 matplotlib 的帧号推进\n        save_count=n_frames,\n        interval=period / 2,         # 半帧轮询，idx 误差 <= 0.5 帧\n        blit=False,\n        repeat=False,\n    )\n    plt.show()\n    return 0\n```\n\nImportant: `ani` referenced inside on_frame before assignment — closure resolves at call time, and callbacks fire after `ani = FuncAnimation(...)` returns. But careful: does FuncAnimation draw the first frame synchronously at construction? No — it draws on timer/`_init_draw` when started by show()/canvas draw event. There is `_init_draw` called when the animation's `_fig` is drawn... In mpl, `Animation.__init__` connects a \"draw_event\"? Actually FuncAnimation's `_init_draw` is called from `_start()` which is called by `plt.show()`? Hmm: `FuncAnimation.__init__` calls `self._fig.canvas.mpl_connect('close_event', ...)` and `self._setup_blit()`? Let me recall:\n\n```python\nclass Animation:\n    def __init__(self, fig, event_source=None, blit=False):\n        ...\n        self._fig.canvas.mpl_connect('close_event', self._handle_close)\n        self._setup_blit()\n```\nand `_setup_blit` connects draw_event and calls `self._init_draw()`? In `_setup_blit`:\n```python\n        self._fig.canvas.mpl_connect('draw_event', self._on_draw)\n```\nand `_start()` triggered from `_on_draw`? For interactive animations, `_start` is called at first draw event.\n\nIn mpl 3.11, `Animation._start` is connected to `fig.canvas` in `_setup_blit`: there's code\n\n```python\n    def _setup_blit(self):\n        # Setting up the blit requires:\n        # 1) an initial draw of the figure\n        # 2) ...\n        self._blit_cache = dict()\n        self._drawn_artists = []\n        self._resize_id = self._fig.canvas.mpl_connect('resize_event', self._on_resize)\n        self._draw_was_started = False\n```\nand `Animation._on_resize`... The start happens in `Animation._start`, connected via `self._fig.canvas.mpl_connect('draw_event', self._on_draw)`? Honestly, the first callback might be invoked synchronously during `plt.show()`; but `ani` is assigned immediately after construction and before `plt.show()`, so by the time any callback runs, `ani` exists. Except if the initial draw happens during `FuncAnimation.__init__` — it does not draw.\n\nStill, to be safe I can avoid referencing `ani` inside the callback by using a holder: but `ani.event_source.stop()` is the clean way. Alternative: `fig.canvas.stop_event_loop()`? Or simply not stop and let it keep polling (wasteful but harmless): it would just keep calling update with clamped last index; with the `i == clock[\"i\"]` early return, it becomes a cheap no-op spin. Actually then the window stays open and idle — that's acceptable. But better to stop.\n\nTo be robust I can use a `nonlocal`-free approach: define `state = {\"t0\": None, \"i\": -1, \"ani\": None}` then `state[\"ani\"] = ani` after construction, and in the callback `if state[\"ani\"] is not None: state[\"ani\"].event_source.stop()`. Slightly uglier. Simpler: just call `plt.get_current_fig_manager()`? No.\n\nI'll keep `ani.event_source.stop()` and note it's safe because callbacks only run after show() starts the timer.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: with `frames=None`, matplotlib's `FuncAnimation.__init__` has:\n\n```python\n        if frames is None:\n            self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n```\nand then `self._frame_gen = self._iter_gen()`? Also `_save_count`... In mpl 3.11 I believe:\n```python\n        if frames is None:\n            self._iter_gen = itertools.count\n        elif callable(frames):\n            self._iter_gen = frames\n        elif isinstance(frames, numbers.Integral):\n            ...\n```\nSo `frames=None` → infinite count → animation never stops on its own; our callback stops it. Good. And `save_count=n_frames` prevents the \"save_count=100\" fallback warning.\n\nAlso `FuncAnimation` passes the frame value from the counter to `on_frame`, we ignore it.\n\nAdditionally: `_draw_was_started`, `blit=False`, so no blit concerns.\n\nAlternatively simpler: keep `frames=itertools.count()` explicit — same. I'll mention it as an equivalent.\n\n**D. Optional but recommended: 让 update 支持\"同一帧不重画\"** — done via `i == clock[\"i\"]` in callback.\n\n**E. line 140 `--fps` type → float**:\n```python\n    p.add_argument(\"--fps\", type=float, default=10.0)\n```\n\nAnd maybe `--speed` scaling for sim time? Not needed.\n\nAlso I should note: total wall time now equals `--duration` regardless of fps/render speed; changing `--fps` only changes angular resolution (how many distinct frames are precomputed) and the polling rate, not the speed. That's exactly the desired decoupling.\n\nAlso note interplay: `interval = period/2` — if fps=10 → 50 ms polling; each poll either draws (if index changed) or returns immediately. So the timer fires ~20/s but draws ~10/s; on a slow machine draws take 200 ms and polls effectively follow draws. Fine.\n\nOne risk: with `interval=period/2` and slow rendering, matplotlib timer callback cannot preempt a draw, so no queue buildup. Fine.\n\nAlso mention: since `t` grid is precomputed at fps resolution, wall-clock index maps to nearest precomputed time; if you want smooth motion under drops, raise `--fps` (only affects precompute cost, not playback speed) — nice property of decoupling. But note TargetMotion precompute for 300 frames is cheap.\n\nNow, also explain *why* this fixes the speed: absolute-time mapping instead of \"one timer tick = one frame\". Include a small formula.\n\nThe user says \"直说修改和添加哪些地方\" — direct. So lead with a numbered list of file:line, then the code blocks, then a 2-line explanation and verification command. Avoid long prose. Also remind chat mode (cannot edit) briefly at the end.\n\nSince I can't run it, I should be careful the code is correct. Let me double check variable shadowing: `on_frame` uses `clock`, `args`, `n_frames`, `out`, `fig`, `ax`, `ax2`, `ax_img`, `ani`. `ani` assigned after def — closure resolves at call time. OK.\n\nAlso `time` imported at module top? I said line 11. Let me confirm current top of file lines 1-20 to give exact insertion point."}
