{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793176206.245271, "id": "2194491c95b9", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_xRkcYAmjRmxroNel7wHP6142", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\": \".venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d\", \"pattern\": \"def set_zscale|def set_box_aspect|def set_aspect\"}"}, {"id": "call_01_YF0yzZ72cFusZqWeqmey9396", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && sed -n '110,122p' scripts/visualize_target.py && sed -n '430,452p' .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/matplotlib/backend_tools.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Confirmed: `Axes.set_xscale` is created by `_axis_method_wrapper(\"xaxis\", \"_set_axes_scale\")` — i.e., a wrapper around `Axis._set_axes_scale`. So there is no `set_scale` on Axes or Axis in mpl 3.11 (public API), only `set_xscale`/`set_yscale` (and `Axes3D.set_zscale`).\n\nAlso `Axes.set_box_aspect` (line 1872) and `set_aspect` (1708).\n\nNow the substantive answer:\n\n1. `set_scale` is not part of the Axes/Axis API in matplotlib 3.11.2 (the only `set_scale` in the installed mpl is `ToolXScale/ToolYScale.set_scale(ax, scale)` — toolbar tools, signature `(self, ax, scale)`, and `toolmanager`. So calling `ax_img.set_scale(...)` → AttributeError. It's not an omission.\n\n2. What people mean by \"set the scale\" for a 2D panel maps onto three different APIs:\n   - data window: `set_xlim/set_ylim` (already at lines 117–118)\n   - pixel aspect: `set_aspect(\"equal\")` (line 119) — the 2D analogue of the 3D `set_box_aspect((1,1,1))` at line 54\n   - axis scale type: `set_xscale/set_yscale` (only \"linear\"/\"log\"/...), meaning数值→位置的映射类型; for pixel u/v that's always linear. And a \"scale\" in the sense of px/m is *derived* from the limits & figure size, there's no API to set it directly — you set the window and the aspect, the scale follows.\n\n3. Key consequence worth flagging (this connects to their speed question): since lines 117-118 recompute the window from the projected bbox **every frame**, the px/m ratio changes per frame → 目标始终占满面板,自转被\"自动缩放\"抵消,看起来又慢又不动。若想要固定比例尺,改成一次固定:\n\n```python\nax_img.set_xlim(0, w)\nax_img.set_ylim(h, 0)     # v 向下\nax_img.set_aspect(\"equal\")  # 必须是 equal,否则 px/m 横向纵向不同\n```\n\n这样 1280×1024 整幅视野,目标只在中间一小块(3 m、0.6 m 直径 ⇒ 约 160 px 宽),但运动/自转是真实速度;要放大就按 fx 同步改,或乘一个固定 zoom 系数:\n\n```python\nzoom = 3\ncx, cy = w / 2, h / 2\nax_img.set_xlim(cx - w / (2*zoom), cx + w / (2*zoom))\nax_img.set_ylim(cy + h / (2*zoom), cy - h / (2*zoom))\n```\n注意 zoom 必须用同一个数作用于两个方向,否则 px/m 会各向异性(除非同时改 aspect)。\n\n4. `set_aspect(\"equal\")` + `set_xlim/set_ylim` 的组合本身就隐含了 autoscale off; 不需要 `set_autoscale_on(False)`,但若依赖 `margins()` 则会重新打开。\n\n5. Also can mention `ax_img.margins(0)` irrelevant since explicit limits.\n\nAlso mention: in 3D, `set_box_aspect` (line 54) is the counterpart of 2D `set_aspect`; there is no `set_scale` there either.\n\nAlso the mermaid? Not necessary but a small table is good.\n\nAlso correct their premise gently: maybe they saw `set_scale` in pan/zoom or in an older API (`backends`? or in `pyplot`? no). Or in `matplotlib.axes.Axes.set_xscale`. Point out that if they meant \"固定缩放比例\", the fix is the fixed limits above.\n\nLet me also double check whether `Axes3D` has `set_zscale`: yes, `Axes3D.set_zscale` exists in mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/axes3d.py. Quick grep to be safe."}
