\"tags\"] = [ax_img.text(0, 0, f\"A{m}\", fontsize=8, zorder=3) for m in range(4)]\n    return art\n```\n\nHmm — the number of armors is hardcoded 4 in build. n_armors is 4 in main. Maybe parameterize `n = 4` and pass? Keep simple: `n_armors = 4` constant in main and pass to build. Since TargetMotion is called with n_armors=4, add `n = 4` local and reference. Simpler to just hardcode 4 and pass `n_armors` to build.\n\nI'll use `n` argument.\n\nNow update function:\n\n```python\ndef draw(index, out, args, art):\n    \"\"\"Point every artist at frame *index*; nothing is created or cleared.\"\"\"\n    centers = out[\"centers\"][0, index].numpy()\n    corners = out[\"corners\"][0, index].numpy()\n    body_rot = out[\"body_rotations\"][0, index].numpy()\n    armor_rot = out[\"armor_rotations\"][0, index].numpy()\n\n    cam_pos = np.array([0.0, 0.0, args.height])\n    body = np.array([args.distance, 0.0, args.height])\n    R_wc = camera_rotation(args.cam_yaw)\n\n    frames = ((np.zeros(3), np.eye(3)), (cam_pos, R_wc.T), (body, body_rot))\n    for lines, (origin, rot) in zip(art[\"frames\"], frames):\n        for line, i in zip(lines, range(3)):\n            line.set_data_3d(*segment(origin, origin + AXIS_LEN * rot[:, i]))\n    ...\n```\n\nLet me define helper:\n\n```python\ndef segment(p0, p1):\n    \"\"\"(3,) + (3,) -> three coordinate arrays for set_data_3d.\"\"\"\n    return np.vstack([p0, p1]).T\n```\nThat gives (3, 2) → `*seg` unpacks 3 arrays of len 2 → correct.\n\nFor 3D frame lengths: world 0.5, camera 0.3, body 0.25 in panel 1; panel 2 body 0.2, armor 0.1. Keep as constants:\n\n```python\nFRAME_LEN = {\"W\": 0.5, \"C\": 0.3, \"B\": 0.25}   # panel 1\nFRAME_LEN2 = (0.2, 0.1)                        # panel 2: body, armor\n```\n\nImplementation for panel 1:\n\n```python\n    for k, (origin, rot, length) in enumerate((\n            (np.zeros(3), np.eye(3), 0.5),\n            (cam_pos, R_wc.T, 0.3),\n            (body, body_rot, 0.25))):\n        for axis_line, j in zip(art[\"frames\"][k], range(3)):\n            axis_line.set_data_3d(*segment(origin, origin + length * rot[:, j]))\n        art[\"labels\"][k].set_position_3d(origin)\n```\n\n`Text3D.set_position_3d(xyz)` — takes 3-tuple. Verified line 167.\n\nThen plates panel 1:\n\n```python\n    for m in range(n):\n        loop = np.vstack([corners[m], corners[m, 0]])\n        art[\"plates\"][m].set_data_3d(*loop.T)\n```\n\nPanel 2:\n\n```python\n    for k, (origin, rot, length) in enumerate(\n            [(body, body_rot, 0.2)] + [(centers[m], armor_rot[m], 0.1) for m in range(n)]):\n        for axis_line, j in zip(art[\"frames2\"][k], range(3)):\n            axis_line.set_data_3d(*segment(origin, origin + length * rot[:, j]))\n        art[\"labels2\"][k].set_position_3d(origin)\n    for m in range(n):\n        art[\"plates2\"][m].set_data_3d(*np.vstack([corners[m], corners[m, 0]]).T)\n```\n\n2D:\n\n```python\n    w, h = args.image_size\n    u, v, _ = project(corners, R_wc, cam_pos, args.fx, args.fx, w / 2, h / 2)\n    normal = np.cross(corners[:, 1] - corners[:, 0], corners[:, 3] - corners[:, 0])\n    normal /= np.linalg.norm(normal, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    front = (normal * (cam_pos - centers)).sum(-1) > 0\n    for m in range(n):\n        loop = np.c_[np.append(u[m], u[m, 0]), np.append(v[m], v[m, 0])]\n        art[\"fills\"][m].set_xy(loop)\n        art[\"fills\"][m].set_visible(bool(front[m]))\n        art[\"edges\"][m].set_data(loop[:, 0], loop[:, 1])\n        art[\"edges\"][m].set_linestyle(\"-\" if front[m] else \":\")\n        art[\"tags\"][m].set_position((u[m].mean(), v[m].mean()))\n```\n\nNote `set_linestyle(\":\")` — works. `front` is np.bool_ → `bool()`.\n\nMain:\n\n```python\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    ... args ...\n    n = 4\n    n_frames = max(2, int(round(args.duration * args.fps)))\n    t = torch.linspace(0.0, args.duration, n_frames).unsqueeze(0)\n    period = 1000.0 / args.fps\n    clock = {\"t0\": None, \"i\": -1}\n\n    out = TargetMotion()(\n        t, \"complex\",\n        p0=torch.tensor([[args.distance, 0.0, args.height]]),\n        yaw_0=torch.tensor([math.radians(args.yaw)]),\n        yaw_rate=torch.tensor([3]),\n        n_armors=n,\n        radius=torch.tensor([args.radius]),\n    )\n\n    w, h = args.image_size\n    cam_pos = np.array([0.0, 0.0, args.height])\n    body = np.array([args.distance, 0.0, args.height])\n    R_wc = camera_rotation(args.cam_yaw)\n    all_corners = out[\"corners\"][0].numpy()              # (T, M, 4, 3)\n    ua, va, _ = project(all_corners, R_wc, cam_pos, args.fx, args.fx, w / 2, h / 2)\n\n    # every frame shares one scale: limits come from the whole trajectory\n    fixed = {\n        \"ax\": cube_limits(np.vstack([all_corners.reshape(-1, 3), cam_pos, np.zeros(3)]), 0.1),\n        \"ax2\": cube_limits(np.vstack([all_corners.reshape(-1, 3), body]), 0.15),\n    }\n    ppm = args.fx / args.distance                 # px per metre at the target\n    half_m = args.radius + 0.25                    # fixed field of view, in metres\n    cu, cv = ua.mean(), va.mean()\n    fixed[\"img\"] = (cu - half_m * ppm, cu + half_m * ppm,\n                    cv + half_m * ppm, cv - half_m * ppm)   # v grows downwards\n\n    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(16.5, 5.2))\n    ax = fig.add_subplot(1, 3, 1, projection=\"3d\")\n    ax2 = fig.add_subplot(1, 3, 2, projection=\"3d\")\n    ax_img = fig.add_subplot(1, 3, 3)\n    fig.subplots_adjust(left=0.04, right=0.98, bottom=0.10, top=0.85, wspace=0.15)\n\n    for a, title in ((ax, \"world, camera and body frames\"),\n                     (ax2, \"armor frames\"),\n                     (ax_img, f\"camera view at {args.distance:.1f} m \"\n                              f\"({w}x{h}, fx={args.fx:.0f}, {ppm:.0f} px/m)\")):\n        a.set_title(title)\n    label_axes(ax)   # hmm, need labels for both 3D panels\n    ...\n    fig.suptitle(\"W world, C camera, B body, A# armor;  x red, y green, z blue\", fontsize=9)\n\n    art = build(args, ax, ax2, ax_img, fixed, n)\n    draw(0, out, args, art, n)\n```\n\nCareful: `label_axes(ax)` sets xlabel/ylabel/zlabel — do for ax and ax2 once.\n\nThen the animation with wall-clock skipping (keep user's existing approach, slightly cleaned):\n\n```python\n    from matplotlib.animation import FuncAnimation\n\n    def on_frame(_frame):\n        if clock[\"t0\"] is None:\n            clock[\"t0\"] = time.perf_counter()\n        i = int((time.perf_counter() - clock[\"t0\"]) / args.duration * n_frames)\n        i = min(i, n_frames - 1)\n        if i == clock[\"i\"]:\n            return\n        clock[\"i\"] = i\n        draw(i, out, args, art, n)\n        if i == n_frames - 1:\n            ani.event_source.stop()\n\n    ani = FuncAnimation(fig, on_frame, frames=None, save_count=n_frames,\n                        interval=period / 2, blit=False, repeat=False)\n    plt.show()\n    return 0\n```\n\nWait: with blit=False and no clear, does the figure actually redraw each frame? Yes: FuncAnimation draws (canvas.draw_idle) after each callback when blit=False.\n\nImportant: `plt.show()` with no `--out`. The user had `--out` argument; previously static saved png. Should we keep `--out`? It's currently unused; unused arg is bad. Options: support `--save` to write a gif? User preference: minimal. But leaving dead `--out` is sloppy. Options:\n- Remove `--out` (it's unused).\n- Or use it: if `--out` given, save via PillowWriter.\n\nHmm, user asked \"按B来改\" — plan B only. Don't add features. But removing `--out` might annoy if they use it. It's unused now, so removing is cleanup. However, my earlier advice mentioned fixed scale etc. I think keep the CLI as is (including --out) or remove. I lean to removing `--out` since it's dead and the script now only shows; but maybe they still want to save the gif later. Better: keep it and make it meaningful? That's scope creep.\n\nDecision: remove `--out` (dead parameter) and note it in the summary. Hmm, but if they have a command line with `--out`, it would error. Risk is low; and dead args are worse. Actually, safer: keep `--out` as an optional save path for the animation: if provided, save GIF with PillowWriter instead of show. That's a couple of lines and makes the arg meaningful... but \"只改 B\" — minimal. And saving requires frames iteration which changes the loop structure (ani.save with wall-clock index doesn't work; frames=None + save is broken). So no.\n\nI'll remove `--out` and mention it. Hmm... Alternatively keep it and ignore — no, dead code.\n\nWait, also `--yaw` is used for yaw_0, `--cam-yaw`, `--fx`, `--image-size`, `--dpi` (dpi unused now! since savefig removed). `--dpi` is also dead. Keep? `fig = plt.figure(figsize=..., dpi=args.dpi)`? That makes dpi meaningful (figure dpi affects rendering resolution & speed). Yes: `plt.figure(figsize=(16.5, 5.2), dpi=args.dpi)`. But dpi=130 at 16.5×5.2 = 2145×676 → heavy per-frame rendering. For animation, dpi 130 is slow; maybe default 100. But the user had 130 for the PNG. I'll pass `dpi=args.dpi` to the figure (making the arg meaningful) — but then default 130 means slow redraw. Since we now update artists only, cost drops a lot. Still, 2145×676 canvas redraw each frame in Agg ~40-60ms. Acceptable-ish at 60fps? No — at fps=60 it can't keep up, but wall-clock skipping handles correctness.\n\nHmm, the user set `--fps` default 60.0. With interval=period/2 = 8.3ms, the timer will fire as fast as draws complete. Fine.\n\nI'll set figure dpi via args.dpi.\n\nAlso `--radius` used. `--height` used. `--distance` used.\n\nNow, one important detail: `draw(0, ...)` initial call before FuncAnimation — good so the initial canvas isn't empty.\n\nAnother: `art[\"fills\"][m].set_visible(front)` — Polygon alpha.\n\nAnother: the `front` computation requires `normal` normalization; degenerate if plate area 0 (never).\n\nAlso `set_data_3d(*loop.T)` where loop is (5,3) → loop.T is (3,5) → unpack 3 arrays len 5. Good.\n\nAlso `segment()` returns (3,2); `*seg` → 3 arrays of len 2. Good.\n\nAnother consideration: `Text3D.set_position_3d(xyz)` — signature `(self, xyz, zdir=None)`. Good.\n\nBut in `build`, `ax.text(0, 0, 0, s, fontsize=8)` returns Text3D.\n\nAlso, `ax.plot([], [], [], ...)` returns list with one Line3D.\n\nAlso autoscale: creating empty lines then `apply_cube` sets limits — order: create artists first, then apply limits, then `ax.set_autoscale_on(False)`. Hmm, `apply_cube` before creating lines: adding lines with `ax.plot([],[],[])` may trigger `_request_autoscale_view()` with empty data → but since we then never add data via plot, autoscale never recomputed? `set_data_3d` doesn't request autoscale, verified. But `_request_autoscale_view` might be called on draw? Autoscale happens in `Axes.draw` if `_autoscaleXon` etc.? In `_AxesBase.draw`, it calls `self._unstale_viewLim()`? Actually there is `self._unstale_viewLim()` in draw which calls `autoscale_view()` if `self._stale_viewlims` — hmm: `_unstale_viewLim` is invoked in `Axes.draw`? Let me recall: `Axes.draw` has:\n\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        ...\n        self._unstale_viewLim()\n```\nYes! `_AxesBase.draw` calls `self._unstale_viewLim()`, which calls `self.autoscale_view(**{f\"{name}scale\": False ...})` for stale axes. If `_stale_viewlims` is set (by `_request_autoscale_view`), then at next draw it re-autoscales, overriding our fixed limits. To be safe, call `set_autoscale_on(False)` on both 3D axes and `ax_img.set_autoscale_on(False)`? For ax_img, explicit set_xlim turns off autoscale for x/y (set_xlim sets `self._autoscaleXon = False`? Let me verify: `_AxesBase.set_xlim` → `self.set_xbound`? Actually `set_xlim` calls `self._set_lim(axis_name=\"x\", v0, v1, ...)` which does `self._autoscaleXon = bool(auto)` with auto=False default? In mpl, `set_xlim(left, right, emit=True, auto=False, ...)` → `self._set_lim(\"x\", v0, v1, auto=auto...)`, and inside `_set_lim`:\n\n```python\n        if auto is not None:\n            self._autoscaleXon = bool(auto)\n```\nHmm signature `def _set_lim(self, axis, v0, v1=None, *, emit=True, auto=False, xmin=None, xmax=None, ymin=None, ymax=None)`. So auto=False → `self._autoscaleXon = False`. Yes, explicit set_xlim disables autoscale.\n\nFor 3D, `Axes3D.set_xlim` inherits `_AxesBase.set_xlim` → also disables autoscale for x. But autoscale for z uses `_autoscaleZon`? Axes3D has `set_zlim`? Yes `Axes3D.set_zlim`? Hmm, in 3D limits are stored in `self._view_lims`? Actually Axes3D inherits `set_zlim` from `_AxesBase`? I believe `Axes3D` has `set_zlim` via `_AxesBase`? There is `set_zlim` in `_AxesBase`? Hmm, `Axes3D.set_zlim` exists (it's in axes3d or base). Anyway the current code calls `ax.set_zlim(...)`, works.\n\nSafest: in `build`, after creating all artists + applying limits, call `ax.set_autoscale_on(False)` and `ax2.set_autoscale_on(False)`. But careful: `set_autoscale_on(False)` sets `_autoscaleXon = _autoscaleYon = False`; for 3D also `_autoscaleZon`? `_AxesBase.set_autoscale_on(b)` sets x/y only. Axes3D overrides? Let me check `axes3d.py` for `set_autoscale_on`... not present. But `Axes3D.autoscale_view` handles z using `_autoscaleZon`? Hmm, `_AxesBase.set_autoscale_on` sets `self._autoscaleXon = b; self._autoscaleYon = b` and maybe `_autoscaleZon` exists in Axes3D only... Let me just also call `ax.set_autoscalex_on(False)`, `set_autoscaley_on(False)`, and for 3D `ax.set_autoscalez_on(False)` if it exists. Hmm complexity.\n\nBetter and simpler: since `set_data_3d` doesn't request autoscale and we never add artists after build, the only autoscale requests happen during `ax.plot`/`text` creation in build. After build we call `apply_cube` → set_xlim/ylim/zlim explicitly, which clears stale viewlims? `set_xlim` calls `self._request_autoscale_view(...)` if auto else `self._stale_viewlims` cleared? In `_set_lim`, after setting, it does:\n\n```python\n        self.stale = True\n```\nand `set_xlim` may call `self._request_autoscale_view(\"x\")`? No — `set_xlim` with auto=False doesn't request. But a *previous* `_request_autoscale_view` (from `plot`) left `self._stale_viewlims[\"x\"] = True`. Does `set_xlim` reset it? In `_AxesBase._set_lim`:\n\n```python\n        if auto is not None:\n            self._autoscaleXon = bool(auto)  # only x\n        ...\n        self.stale = True\n```\nHmm, and `_stale_viewlims` reset happens in `_unstale_viewLim` when drawn: it calls `self.autoscale_view(**{f\"{name}scale\": False ...})`, which computes limits only for those with `_autoscaleXon` True AND stale. Since autoscale is off, `autoscale_view` won't change limits? Let me look: `autoscale_view(scalex=True...)` → computes `x0, x1 = self.xaxis.get_margins()` from dataLim and calls `self._set_lim(\"x\", x0, x1, auto=None)`? If `_autoscaleXon` False, `autoscale_view` still... hmm, `_AxesBase.autoscale_view`:\n\n```python\n    def autoscale_view(self, tight=None, scalex=True, scaley=True, scalez=True):\n        if tight is None:\n            _tight = self._tight\n            ...\n        if scalex and self._autoscaleXon:\n            ...\n```\nYes it checks `_autoscaleXon`. So with autoscale off, `_unstale_viewLim` calling `autoscale_view` will be a no-op for x/y. For z (3D), `Axes3D.autoscale_view`? Hmm, in mpl 3.11, `_AxesBase.autoscale_view` handles z too (`scalez` param). And it checks `self._autoscaleZon`? Let me check the actual code to be safe. Let me grep `_autoscaleZon` in _base.py.\n\nGiven uncertainty, include explicit autoscale-off calls in `build`:\n```python\n    for a in (ax, ax2):\n        a.set_autoscale_on(False)   # x, y (3D also honours this for x/y)\n```\nand check `_autoscaleZon`. Let me grep.\n\nAlso, there's a subtle known 3D behavior: `Axes3D` autoscales z on draw via `zz_dataLim`; `set_zlim` sets `self._autoscaleZon = False`? Let me verify.\n\nLet me grep both."}
