: print the u/v bbox of all plates and the window (get_xlim), to see the ratio. From run 1: window = cu ± 147, cv ± 147. u_all spans? Let me compute quickly with a script: pts bbox width over all frames at 3 m: cluster diameter 0.6 m → 160 px; plate half-width 0.06 → +16 px each side → ~192 px. Window 294 px → 65% used. Fine.\n\nBut ASCII shows content only in middle ~2 rows — vertically the plates are 16 px tall out of 294 px window → 5% of height. So a lot of vertical whitespace. That's inherent: the target is a ring of plates with zero height offsets, so vertical extent tiny. The old auto-fit window had the same issue (it padded 10%).\n\nCould improve by using an anisotropic window (wider than tall) — but then `set_aspect(\"equal\")` with adjustable box would shrink. Better: window based on projected bbox of all frames with aspect equal: `half_w = max(ptp(u), ptp(v))/2 + margin` → same tiny height. To make it look nicer, we could set a window with a fixed *vertical* half of e.g. 0.15 m and horizontal half 0.4 m, but equal aspect would then letterbox the axes box (empty space), which is normal (like the old figure).\n\nThe current default (radius+0.25 = 0.55 m half-window) gives a square 0.55×0.55 m FOV → 294×294 px data → plates (0.6 m wide, 0.06 m tall) fill width but not height. That's physically honest. Since plates are at a single height, maybe reduce half to radius+0.15 = 0.45 m? Then window 240 px; cluster 192 px → 80% width. Slightly tighter.\n\nMinor aesthetic; keep `radius + 0.25`? The ASCII shows a lot of empty space but that's the geometry. I could add a `--view` arg (half-field in metres, default 0). Hmm, user prefers minimal. Keep.\n\nHmm wait, but actually there's an important observation from the ASCII: panel 3 grid shows many ticks across a huge range — the numbers might be strange because data are pixels; fine.\n\nNow, the more important measurement: per-frame draw 98 ms at dpi 100. With the artist-reuse version the update cost is small, but full