{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793176773.078894, "id": "72d8a1177340", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_l09nTCaui3PioZSjblx81932", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && cat > /tmp/check_draw.py <<\\\"PYEOF\\\"\\nimport sys\\nimport matplotlib\\nmatplotlib.use(\\\"Agg\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nimport matplotlib.animation as manim\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\".\\\")\\nimport scripts.visualize_target as vis\\n\\nCAP = {}\\nclass Cap(manim.FuncAnimation):\\n    def __init__(self, *a, **k):\\n        super().__init__(*a, **k)\\n        CAP[\\\"ani\\\"] = self\\nmanim.FuncAnimation = Cap\\n\\ndef snap(fig):\\n    ax, ax2, ax_img = fig.axes\\n    return np.array([*ax.get_xlim(), *ax.get_ylim(), *ax.get_zlim(),\\n                     *ax2.get_xlim(), *ax2.get_ylim(), *ax2.get_zlim(),\\n                     *ax_img.get_xlim(), *ax_img.get_ylim()], float)\\n\\ndef fake_show(*a, **k):\\n    fig = plt.gcf()\\n    ani = CAP[\\\"ani\\\"]\\n    clock = None\\n    for cell in (ani._func.__closure__ or ()):\\n        try:\\n            v = cell.cell_contents\\n        except ValueError:\\n            continue\\n        if isinstance(v, dict) and \\\"t0\\\" in v:\\n            clock = v\\n    before = snap(fig)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    after = snap(fig)\\n    for k in (0, 15, 30, 45):\\n        clock[\\\"i\\\"] = -1\\n        clock[\\\"t0\\\"] = __import__(\\\"time\\\").perf_counter() - k * 0.04\\n        ani._func(0)\\n        fig.canvas.draw()\\n    later = snap(fig)\\n    print(\\\"limits unchanged by draw:\\\", np.allclose(before, after))\\n    print(\\\"limits unchanged across frames + draws:\\\", np.allclose(before, later))\\n    print(\\\"window px:\\\", np.round(after[12:16], 2))\\n    print(\\\"axis artists:\\\", len(fig.axes[0].lines), len(fig.axes[0].texts),\\n          len(fig.axes[2].patches), len(fig.axes[2].texts))\\n\\nplt.show = fake_show\\nvis.main([\\\"--duration\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"--fps\\\", \\\"50\\\"])\\nPYEOF\\n.venv/bin/python /tmp/check_draw.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "All geometry correct:\n- frame axis lengths 0.5/0.3/0.25 (panel 1), 0.2/0.1 (panel 2)\n- plate loop 5 points, 3 unique (closed loop with 42 dupes? \"unique pts: 3\" — hmm: a plate has 4 unique corners + repeat = 5. It says 3 unique?! That's suspicious.\n\nWait: `np.unique(loop.round(6), axis=0)` on shape (3,5) — loop is (3,5) because `get_data_3d` returns (3, N)! I printed shape (3,5) — that's (x,y,z rows). So unique along axis=0 counts unique *coordinate rows*, not points. My mistake. The w/h computed with `loop[:,1]-loop[:,0]` — loop is (3,5) so loop[:, j] is a column = point j across x,y,z rows. So `np.linalg.norm(loop[:,1]-loop[:,0])` = distance between point 1 and point 0 = width 0.12 ✓, and point2 - point1 = height 0.06 ✓. Good. unique(axis=0) meaningless. Fine.\n\n- texts correct.\n- colored pixels present in all 3 panels.\n\nSo the refactor works: fixed limits, no accumulation, artists update, wall-clock skipping.\n\nRemaining concerns:\n1. ~92-98 ms per full redraw → ~10 fps ceiling. Should I reduce default fps from 60 to something like 30? Playback is wall-clock limited anyway; `--fps` only affects precomputed temporal resolution. With fps=60 and duration=10 → 600 frames precomputed (cheap). Keep. But the interval = 500/fps = 8.3 ms → timer fires ~120 Hz trying to draw, each draw 92 ms → effectively draw-bound. Fine; maybe interval should be `1000/fps` (16.6 ms) to avoid pointless callbacks? Timer callbacks that return early are cheap (one perf_counter call), but 120/s of no-ops is negligible. Keep half-period for finer index quantization.\n\n2. `plt.show()` blocking; nothing saved. `--out` removed — mention.\n\n3. Should I keep `--dpi` default 100 (was 130 with savefig). Mention.\n\nNow let me also test the actual `plt.show()` path won't crash on a real backend... can't. But `ani` is referenced inside on_frame; with frames=None the timer starts on first draw. Fine.\n\nOne risk: with `frames=None` and `save_count=n_frames`, `FuncAnimation.__init__` may warn/ignore. In our headless run we never drew the first frame via the timer, but we constructed FuncAnimation without warnings (no warnings printed). Good.\n\nAnother risk: `plt.show()` in a GUI backend starts the timer; after the last frame we call `ani.event_source.stop()`. Fine.\n\nAnother: `draw(0, ...)` before show ensures the first frame is painted immediately.\n\nAnother: if the user closes the window, timer stops.\n\nLet me also double check that `apply_cube` with `set_zlim` on 3D and `set_autoscale_on(False)` produce stable limits *after* a real canvas draw (autoscale could be re-triggered at first draw). Our harness did `fig.canvas.draw()` after stepping frames and limits stayed stable? I checked limits across `f()` calls but not after `canvas.draw()`. `_unstale_viewLim` runs in `Axes.draw` and could autoscale if flags stale — but autoscale off, so no. Let me verify by comparing limits before/after `canvas.draw()` in the harness. Quick test.\n\nAlso verify that after the *first* real draw, artist data isn't reset.\n\nLet me run a small test: capture limits before draw and after draw."}
