ier it worked.\n\nLet me be careful: I should answer the question with analysis. Ideally with measurements to be concrete. I can profile where the 94 ms goes: 3D axes drawing dominates.\n\nWhat can be done to reduce per-frame latency:\n\n1. Diagnosis: total redraw ~94 ms for 1650×520 px. Where does it go? Likely:\n   - Axes3D.draw: each axis does projection, pane rendering (Poly3DCollection), gridlines, tick labels, axis lines... Axes3D drawing is notoriously slow; two Axes3D panels dominate.\n   - Also `fig.canvas.draw()` in TkAgg blits image to screen.\n\n2. Options to reduce per-frame cost (ordered by payoff/effort):\n   a. **Drop one 3D panel** (armor close-up) → maybe -40%.\n   b. **Disable 3D pane painting** (`ax.xaxis.pane.set_visible(False)` / `ax.set_axis_off()`), remove grid (`ax.grid(False)`), remove tick labels — Axes3D spends a lot in rendering panes, gridlines, and tick text. Also `ax.tick_params(...)` no-op. Setting `ax.set_axis_off()` on 3D axes removes ticks/labels/panes but keeps lines → big win.\n   c. **Reduce the number of 3D artists**: each `Line3D.do_3d_projection` is cheap; the expensive part is mpl's draw pipeline (per-artist state, path creation, Agg rendering of many path segments). We have 13 + 19 + 4 lines: ~36 lines. Not huge.\n   d. **Use `blit=True`** in FuncAnimation with a background: for 2D axes blitting works; for 3D it also can work (artists' `set_visible`/data changes + `ax.draw_artist`). Blitting avoids full canvas redraw: instead of 94 ms full draw, redrawing only artists could be ~10-20 ms. But the 2D image panel uses a fixed scale so blitting is safe (no autoscale, no moving). Yet 3D artists draws still require `Axes3D.draw`? With blit, mpl draws background once and per frame calls `ax.draw_artist(artist)` for each artist in `_drawn_artists` — for 3D artists, `Line3D.draw` requires the axes projection matrix `self.axes.M` to be current; that's set during `Axes3D.draw`. It's usually still set from the background draw, so it works (a known trick: 3D blitting works if view (elev/azim) doesn't change). Text3D also. Polygon in 2D fine.\n      Risk: 3D blitting can be buggy (text/pane). Payoff: potentially 5-10x.\n   e. **Lower the number of drawn points**: plates are 5 points; axis lines 2 points. Negligible.\n   f. **Reduce figure size / dpi**: previously measured no difference (Agg path costs dominate). So not it.\n   g. **Avoid `set_visible`/`set_linestyle` churn**? `set_linestyle` triggers a path regeneration? Setting linestyle each frame marks stale and recreates `_dash_pattern`; cheap.\n   h. **Only update every other frame** — that's the wall-clock skip that already exists.\n   i. Cache: reuse `Path` objects? Not controllable.\n   j. Use `fig.canvas.draw_idle()`? Not helpful for FuncAnimation.\n   k. Reduce 3D tick/axis overhead: `ax.set_axis_off()` + manual labels; `ax.grid(False)`.\n   l. Set `matplotlib.rcParams['path.simplify']`... negligible.\n   m. Replace the 3D panels with 2D projections computed by hand (the orthographic projection is 10 lines of numpy) → drawing 2D lines is ~10x cheaper than Axes3D. That's the real \"engineering\" answer: the panels are just lines; a 2D axes with precomputed projected coordinates draws in ~2-5 ms. But that's a rewrite removing the \"3D axis with panes/ticks\" look.\n   n. Use `Agg` + `blit` or draw to an image and blit manually.\n\nBest measurement first: profile. Let me actually measure, since I can run commands (they worked before). Let me write a profiling script in /tmp that:\n   - runs the module headless via runpy,\n   - times `fig.canvas.draw()` (full),\n   - times just `ax_world.draw(renderer)` vs `ax_armor.draw` vs `ax_image.draw`,\n   - times `update(index)` alone,\n   - tries variants: axis off, grid off, pane invisible.\n\nThen report numbers and recommend options with estimated gains, ideally measured.\n\nGiven chat mode read-only, writing /tmp files is technically a write... but it's outside the workspace, and earlier runs succeeded. The instruction says never modify the workspace; /tmp scratch is arguably fine and needed to measure honestly. The earlier turns already did that. However, the mode also says run_command rejects anything that could write files. My previous `cat > /tmp/...` heredocs worked. I'll do targeted measurement with a single python script via `-c`? `-c` with long code is unwieldy; better use a heredoc into `.venv/bin/python -` (stdin), which doesn't create files at all! `python - <<PYEOF` — that avoids writing files. Note the earlier constraint: heredoc content must not contain single quotes (parse issue). I'll avoid `'` characters (use double quotes).\n\nPlan measurement script (stdin):\n1. runpy the script with Agg + patched show/FuncAnimation (need runpy... fine, no file writes).\n2. Time:\n   - `fig.canvas.draw()` 3-5 times (median).\n   - Per-axes: create a renderer via `fig.canvas.get_renderer()`, `t=perf_counter(); ax.draw(renderer)`.\n   - `update(i)` alone (no drawing).\n3. Variants on a copy of the figure? Simplest: modify g (globals) — e.g. call `ax_world.set_axis_off()` then re-time `ax_world.draw(renderer)`; `ax_world.grid(False)`; `pane.set_visible(False)`.\n4. Also time with `blit`-style: `ax.draw_artist(line)`? Hard to replicate; but I can measure \"redraw only artists via draw_artist\" roughly: for the image axes: `ax_img.draw_artist(line)`.\n\nAlso measure a 2D-only variant estimate: build a fig with 3 subplots all 2D and time draw.\n\nLet me write the measurement carefully, avoiding single quotes.\n\nKey questions to answer numerically:\n- How much of the 94 ms is each panel?\n- How much does `set_axis_off()` / pane-invisible / grid-off save?\n- How much would blitting save (measure background + per-frame draw_artist sum)?\n- How much would converting 3D panels to 2D projections save?\n\nLet me do it.\n\nNote: Agg vs TkAgg drawing cost is similar for the artist rendering; TkAgg adds image blit to screen (~few ms).\n\nScript outline:\n\n```python\nimport os, sys, time, runpy\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport matplotlib.animation as manim\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\nmanim.FuncAnimation = type(\"Cap\", (manim.FuncAnimation,), {})\n\ng = runpy.run_path(\"scripts/visualize_target.py\", run_name=\"__main__\")\nfig = g[\"fig\"]; update = g[\"update\"]; n = g[\"N_FRAMES\"]\naxw, axa, axi = g[\"ax_world\"], g[\"ax_armor\"], g[\"ax_image\"]\n\ndef timeit(fn, k=5):\n    ts = []\n    for _ in range(k):\n        t = time.perf_counter(); fn(); ts.append(time.perf_counter() - t)\n    return sorted(ts)[len(ts)//2] * 1e3\n\nprint(\"update(i) only: %.2f ms\" % timeit(lambda: [update(i % n) for i in range(0, 6)]))\n```\n\nHmm, better: measure update for a single frame but it's dominated by python overhead; do 60 updates / 60.\n\n```python\nprint(\"full canvas.draw: %.1f ms\" % timeit(lambda: fig.canvas.draw()))\nrenderer = fig.canvas.get_renderer()\nprint(\"ax_world.draw: %.1f ms\" % timeit(lambda: axw.draw(renderer)))\nprint(\"ax_armor.draw: %.1f ms\" % timeit(lambda: axa.draw(renderer)))\nprint(\"ax_image.draw: %.1f ms\" % timeit(lambda: axi.draw(renderer)))\n```\n\nThen variants:\n```python\nfor ax in (axw, axa): ax.grid(False)\n```\nHmm, Axes3D grid lines are drawn via `_axinfo`/grid; `ax.grid(False)` should disable. Then time again.\n\nThen panes:\n```python\nfor axis in (axw.xaxis, axw.yaxis, axw.zaxis): axis.pane.set_visible(False)\n```\nActually `axw.xaxis.pane.fill = False` and `.set_edgecolor(\"none\")`. In mpl 3.11, `Axis3D` has `pane` (Patch3D). `ax.set_axis_off()` is the bluntest: hides everything including axis labels/ticks/panes? For 3D, `set_axis_off` makes `ax._axis3don = False`? There's `Axes3D.set_axis_off()` which sets `self._axis3don = False` (in older versions). In 3.11: `def set_axis_off(self): self._axis3don = False`? Let me check. And `ax.set_axis_on()`.\n\nMeasure: `ax.set_axis_off()` for both 3D axes then time each and full draw.\n\nThen estimate blit path: \n```python\nfig.canvas.draw()\nbg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\nt = time.perf_counter()\nfor _ in range(5):\n    for ax in (axw, axa):\n        for line in ax.lines: ax.draw_artist(line)\n    for t_ in axw.texts: axw.draw_artist(t_)  # hmm Text3D\n    axi.draw_artist(...)\nfig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\n```\nThis is doable but fiddly; and Agg blit works (copy_from_bbox). Let me measure \"draw only the artists\" without blit to see painter cost:\n\n```python\nartists = [*axw.lines, *axw.texts, *axa.lines, *axa.texts, *axi.lines, *axi.patches, *axi.texts]\nt=...; for a in artists: a.axes.draw_artist(a)\n```\nThat gives lower bound for blit-based redraw.\n\nCareful: 3D artists need projection matrix set (from the background full draw) — it is, since we drew once.\n\nThen a \"2D proxy\" measurement: how long does a 2D axes with 36 Line2D draw? Build a test figure with 3 2D axes, put the same-ish number of lines, and time draw. That estimates the ceiling of \"convert 3D panels to 2D projection\" approach.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe a large part of the cost is the `Polygon` patches with antialiasing? no.\n\nAnother important factor: Agg's draw of text (tick labels) — many ticks × 3 axes. 3D axes have 3 axes × ~6 ticks with labels + offset text. Font rendering is expensive (~0.2-0.5 ms per string?). Actually Agg text rendering is not that expensive but adds up.\n\nLet me run the measurements and then give concrete advice.\n\nAlso worth checking: does the user perceive \"frame-to-frame computation latency\" as the ~94 ms per redraw? Yes. Goal: lower to e.g. 15-30 ms for 30-60 fps.\n\nExciting option: blit=True with 3D. Let's test whether it actually works in mpl 3.11 for Line3D/Text3D. Known issue: 3D artists with blit require `ax.M` set; and Text3D's `do_3d_projection` needs `self.axes.M`. Works after a full draw. Known matplotlib issue #12361? People do use blit with 3D... Let me test: patch the module version? I can test by constructing a FuncAnimation with blit=True and driving frames via `ani._step()`? That mutates. Simpler: replicate blit logic manually in the measurement script:\n\n```python\nfig.canvas.draw()\nbg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\n# per frame: update(index); restore bg; draw artists; blit\n```\nMeasure the per-frame time of that loop. If it works and is fast, recommend it and (in work mode) implement it.\n\nBut note: the user is in chat mode now? I should answer the analysis question and offer to implement. The tools did permit edits earlier... but the system prompt explicitly says chat mode = read-only, don't modify. The user's question is \"can we further optimize?\" — an analysis question. Best: measure, present options with numbers, and ask if they want me to implement (or say \"switch to work mode and I'll do it\"). Actually since previous turns had me editing in this same conversation, mode may have been work. But the current system prompt says chat (read-only). I'll present analysis + concrete patch, and note that implementing needs work mode.\n\nHmm, but it'd be much better to give them the actual measured best option. Let me measure thoroughly, then present the recommended patch as a diff in the answer (not applying it), and mention switching to work mode to apply.\n\nLet's do the measurement now. Avoid single quotes in the heredoc.\n\nCareful with `fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox` — Agg supports it (`FigureCanvasAgg.copy_from_bbox`). For TkAgg, `copy_from_bbox`/`blit` also supported (Agg-based). Good.\n\nLet me write the script:\n\n```python\nimport os, sys, time, runpy\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport matplotlib.animation as manim\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\nmanim.FuncAnimation = type(\"Cap\", (manim.FuncAnimation,), {})\n\ng = runpy.run_path(\"scripts/visualize_target.py\", run_name=\"__main__\")\nfig, update, n = g[\"fig\"], g[\"update\"], g[\"N_FRAMES\"]\naxw, axa, axi = g[\"ax_world\"], g[\"ax_armor\"], g[\"ax_image\"]\nrenderer = fig.canvas.get_renderer()\n\ndef med(fn, k=7):\n    ts = []\n    for _ in range(k):\n        t0 = time.perf_counter(); fn(); ts.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n    ts.sort()\n    return ts[len(ts)//2] * 1e3\n\ndef upd(k=60):\n    for i in range(k):\n        update(i)\n\nprint(\"update() per frame:      %.3f ms\" % (med(upd, 3) / 60))\nprint(\"full canvas.draw:        %.1f ms\" % med(fig.canvas.draw))\nprint(\"  ax_world.draw:         %.1f ms\" % med(lambda: axw.draw(renderer)))\nprint(\"  ax_armor.draw:         %.1f ms\" % med(lambda: axa.draw(renderer)))\nprint(\"  ax_image.draw:         %.1f ms\" % med(lambda: axi.draw(renderer)))\n```\n\nWait: `ax.draw(renderer)` alone doesn't set the figure's dpi/DPI? It's fine.\n\nThen variants:\n\n```python\nfor ax in (axw, axa):\n    ax.grid(False)\nprint(\"with 3D grid off:        %.1f ms\" % med(fig.canvas.draw))\n```\n\nGrid off might not affect much; the 3D panes are separate patches.\n\n```python\nfor ax in (axw, axa):\n    for axis in (ax.xaxis, ax.yaxis, ax.zaxis):\n        axis.pane.set_visible(False)\nprint(\"+ panes hidden:          %.1f ms\" % med(fig.canvas.draw))\n```\nHmm, Axis3D `pane` attribute: in mpl 3.11, `Axis3D` has `pane` property? There is `self.pane = ...`? In axes3d `_init3d`, `self.set_pane_color`? Let me check: Axis3D has `self.pane` (a Patch3D) and `self.line`. It also has `_axinfo`. In 3.11, `Axis3D.__init__` sets `self.pane = mpatches.Polygon(...)`? I recall `self.pane = mpatches.Polygon(..., facecolor=..., ...)`. Something like that; also `self._axinfo`. I'll guard with try/except.\n\nSimplest robust blunt variant: `ax.set_axis_off()`:\n```python\nfor ax in (axw, axa):\n    ax.set_axis_off()\nprint(\"with 3D axes off:        %.1f ms\" % med(fig.canvas.draw))\n```\nDoes `Axes3D.set_axis_off` exist and drop ticks? `Axis3D` drawing is driven by `self._axis3don`. In 3.11, `Axes3D.set_axis_off()` sets `self._axis3don = False`. Let me verify via grep.\n\nThen measure per-axes again with axis off to see the split.\n\nThen blit estimate:\n\n```python\nfig.canvas.draw()\nbg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\narts = [*axw.lines, *axw.texts, *axa.lines, *axa.texts, *axi.lines, *axi.patches, *axi.texts]\nprint(\"artists:\", len(arts))\n\ndef blit_frame(i):\n    update(i)\n    fig.canvas.restore_region(bg)\n    for a in arts:\n        a.axes.draw_artist(a)\n    fig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\n\nprint(\"blit frame (3D artists): %.1f ms\" % med(lambda: blit_frame(3)))\n```\nNote: to be fair the index should change; use a mutable counter.\n\nAlso test blit with axis_off 3D? Later.\n\nAlso test the \"convert to 2D\" ceiling:\n\n```python\nfig2 = plt.figure(figsize=(16.5, 5.2))\na2 = [fig2.add_subplot(1, 3, i + 1) for i in range(3)]\nfor ax in a2:\n    for _ in range(13):\n        ax.plot(np.random.rand(5), np.random.rand(5))\n    ax.set_xlim(0, 1); ax.set_ylim(0, 1); ax.set_autoscale_on(False)\nprint(\"3 x 2D axes, 13 lines each: %.1f ms\" % med(lambda: fig2.canvas.draw()))\n```\nThis gives the ceiling for a hand-rolled 2D projection approach (no panes/ticks? they'd have ticks: default 2D axes have ticks/labels too).\n\nAlso worth measuring: 2D axes draw cost without ticks (`set_axis_off`).\n\nLet me run it in stages to avoid one huge script failing. I'll do it in one script with try/except around the fiddly bits.\n\nAlso: measure `fig.canvas.draw()` when only the 2D panel is present? Not needed if per-axes draw adds up.\n\nOne caveat: `ax.draw(renderer)` three times adds up to ~full draw? Full draw also includes figure patch, suptitle, and canvas blit. Should roughly match.\n\nLet me run.\n\nAlso check `Axes3D.set_axis_off` existence first."}
