{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793177995.6172743, "id": "4afcedf3ef64", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_hjVqOZ8mk7gcFFal35Gd4873", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && sed -n '265,345p' .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/art3d.py && echo ---- && sed -n '120,200p' .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/art3d.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Work mode now. Let's measure the baseline breakdown first (write a /tmp script), then implement blit and measure after.\n\nMeasurement script (/tmp/prof.py):\n- runpy the module with Agg, patched show & FuncAnimation capture.\n- time full draw, per-axes draw, update.\n- variants: grid off, panes hidden, set_axis_off.\n- blit simulation: background + restore + draw_artist for moving artists + blit.\n\nLet me write it avoiding single quotes issue (I'll use write_file into /tmp — write_file path is workspace-relative only? \"path is relative to the workspace root\" — so /tmp writes go via run_command heredoc (no `'` chars) or via python. Earlier I used `cat > /tmp/... <<\"PYEOF\"` successfully with single quotes inside? Earlier /tmp/check_simple.py was written with write_file path \"/tmp/check_simple.py\" and it worked! Yes — write_file accepted /tmp/check_simple.py. Great, use write_file for /tmp scripts (absolute outside workspace allowed apparently).\n\nWait, instructions say paths relative to workspace root; but /tmp worked. I'll use it again.\n\nScript /tmp/prof.py:\n\n```python\nimport os, sys, time, runpy\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport matplotlib.animation as manim\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\nCAP = {}\n\nclass Cap(manim.FuncAnimation):\n    def __init__(self, *a, **k):\n        super().__init__(*a, **k)\n        CAP[\"ani\"] = self\n        CAP[\"blit\"] = k.get(\"blit\", False)\n\nmanim.FuncAnimation = Cap\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\n\ng = runpy.run_path(\"scripts/visualize_target.py\", run_name=\"__main__\")\nfig, update, n = g[\"fig\"], g[\"update\"], g[\"N_FRAMES\"]\naxw, axa, axi = g[\"ax_world\"], g[\"ax_armor\"], g[\"ax_image\"]\nrenderer = fig.canvas.get_renderer()\n\ndef med(fn, k=7):\n    ts = []\n    for _ in range(k):\n        t0 = time.perf_counter(); fn(); ts.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n    ts.sort()\n    return ts[len(ts) // 2] * 1e3\n\ndef upd(k=60):\n    for i in range(k):\n        update(i)\n\nprint(\"CAP blit =\", CAP.get(\"blit\"))\nprint(\"update data only   %6.2f ms\" % (med(upd, 3) / 60))\nprint(\"full canvas.draw   %6.1f ms\" % med(fig.canvas.draw))\nprint(\"  ax_world.draw    %6.1f ms\" % med(lambda: axw.draw(renderer)))\nprint(\"  ax_armor.draw    %6.1f ms\" % med(lambda: axa.draw(renderer)))\nprint(\"  ax_image.draw    %6.1f ms\" % med(lambda: axi.draw(renderer)))\n```\n\nThen blit simulation. Need moving artists list. For measuring the \"after\" state let me just import the module's `moving` list if present (after my edit). For a generic profiling script, compute from globals: `g.get(\"moving\")` else build.\n\nSimulate blit frame:\n```python\nfig.canvas.draw()\nbg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\narts = g[\"moving\"]\nstate = {\"i\": 0}\ndef frame():\n    state[\"i\"] = (state[\"i\"] + 1) % n\n    update(state[\"i\"])\n    fig.canvas.restore_region(bg)\n    for a in arts:\n        a.axes.draw_artist(a)\n    fig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\nprint(\"blit frame         %6.1f ms\" % med(frame))\n```\n\nCareful: for Text3D artists, `a.axes.draw_artist(a)` works? `draw_artist` checks `artist.axes is self` (for `Axes.draw_artist`) — yes since we call `a.axes.draw_artist(a)`. It also handles clip. Text3D.draw needs `self._x,self._y` set by `do_3d_projection`? Text3D.draw calls `super().draw(renderer)` after checking `self._x`... In art3d.Text3D.draw:\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        # docstring inherited\n        if not self.get_visible():\n            return\n        # update the projected coordinates\n        self._update_position()? \n```\nHmm, in mpl 3.11 Text3D.draw does:\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        # docstring inherited\n        if not self.get_visible():\n            return\n        if self._d3d_html? \n```\nI recall:\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        # docstring inherited\n        if not self.get_visible():\n            return\n        # TODO: These don't have any effect within the 3D axes ...\n        self._update_position_xy2d()   # something like this\n        super().draw(renderer)\n```\nLet me check art3d.Text3D around line 167-200 to see if `draw` exists and whether it needs `self.axes.M`. If `do_3d_projection` must be called before draw, blit would break for text (positions stale). Let me check the source.\n\nAlso Line3D.draw: \n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        # docstring inherited\n        self._set_unstale()? \n```\nHmm, Line3D.draw:\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        # docstring inherited\n        if not self.get_visible():\n            return\n        # Line2D.draw is called by super()\n        super().draw(renderer)\n```\nAnd Line3D data are already projected? No — Line3D keeps 3D coords and `do_3d_projection` computes `self._xs, self._ys` etc. through `_proj_transform`? Let's look: Line3D.do_3d_projection:\n```python\n    def do_3d_projection(self):\n        xs3d, ys3d, zs3d = self._verts3d\n        xs, ys, zs = proj3d.proj_transform(xs3d, ys3d, zs3d, self.axes.M)\n        self.set_data(xs, ys)\n        return np.min(zs)\n```\nSo it calls set_data → the 2D data updated. Who calls do_3d_projection? `Axes3D.draw` only for collections/patches (see source above), and for lines? In `Axes3D.draw`, after `super().draw(renderer)`, the children are drawn by `_AxesBase.draw` which calls `artist.draw(renderer)`; for Line3D.draw:\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        # docstring inherited\n        if not self.get_visible():\n            return\n        # ...\n```\nHmm, need to check. Let me grep art3d for \"def draw\" in Line3D/Text3D.\n\nThis matters a lot for blit feasibility: if `Line3D.draw` itself triggers the projection (likely it does, calling `self.do_3d_projection()` — I think in newer mpl `Line3D.draw` isn't overridden and instead `Axes3D.draw` iterates `self._children`... hmm).\n\nLet me read art3d.py around Line3D (265-330) and Text3D."}
