{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793178031.4602575, "id": "96d1104b74d5", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_waj1lqrruHKj5RImdDy28337", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\": 30, \"offset\": 183, \"path\": \"scripts/visualize_target.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Baseline numbers:\n- `update()`: 0.42 ms → negligible.\n- full draw: 95.7 ms\n- ax_world 31.3, ax_armor 37.2, ax_image 20.7 (sum 89)\n- artists only: 12.7 ms\n- blit frame: 13.7 ms → **7× faster**\n- grid off: 87 (small)\n- panes hidden: 87 (small — surprising, pane drawing cheap?)\n- axis off: 43.7 (2.2× faster; both 3D axes down to 8.7/10.0) → the axis decoration (ticks+labels+grid lines via Axis3D.draw) costs ~22 ms per panel; panes themselves cheap.\n- axis off + blit: 13.3 (same as blit) → blit dominates.\n\nInteresting: ax_image 20.7 ms is also sizable. artists only 12.7 includes all three panels' artists. So blit ~13.7 ms.\n\nSo: blit gives 95.7 → 13.7 ms (≈7×). That's the big win. Implement blit=True.\n\nCan we do better? The remaining 13.7 ms = 12.7 artists + 1 ms blit/restore. Artists: 52 artists; the 3D ones reproject in draw (Line3D.draw does proj3d transform each time) — cheap. The Agg drawing of each artist has fixed overhead.\n\nCould we cut further? Options: fewer artists (combine lines into fewer paths) — e.g. draw all 3 axis segments of a triad as one Line3D with NaN separators → 3×→1 artist. That would cut 52 → ~30 artists. Possible but adds complexity (NaN separators). Also the 2D image panel: 4 fills + 4 edges + 4 tags.\n\nLet me check: is 13.7 ms good enough? 70 fps. Yes, plenty. Don't over-engineer; the user wants minimal.\n\nBut note: with blit=True, the displayed fps is limited by whom? interval=1000/60=16.7 ms → each timer tick triggers a blit frame of ~14 ms → ~40-60 fps achievable. Actual achievable fps = 1/(14+overhead) ≈ 70 fps, so timer at 60 fps is fine.\n\nWait: also TkAgg blit to screen adds cost (~1-3 ms). Let me verify with the real backend by measuring the actual frame rate: run the real script with SECONDS=2 and count how many `update` calls happen, or measure loop time. Better: instrument by measuring how many times animate is called in N seconds with the real backend. Could patch... simpler: after implementing blit, run the real script (tkagg) with a monkeypatched counter via a wrapper script in /tmp that imports and counts? Since it's a top-level script, use runpy with `plt.show` real (blocks). Hmm.\n\nAlternative: measure per-frame cost with the tkagg backend canvas but without showing: `plt.switch_backend(\"tkagg\")` requires display; runpy the module with a patched `plt.show` that runs a loop for 2 s measuring actual animate+blit frame times using the real canvas (draw_artist + blit on tkagg canvas). That measures real per-frame cost including window blit. Let me do that: copy prof.py but with backend tkagg and only the blit measurement (using fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox which TkAgg supports).\n\nLet me implement blit in the script first, then measure both Agg and TkAgg.\n\nImplementation in the module:\n\n```python\n# every artist that moves, so blitting can repaint just those\nmoving = ([a for lines, text, _ in world_frames + armor_frames\n           for a in (*lines, text)]\n          + world_plates + armor_plates + fills + edges + tags)\n```\nPlace right before `update()` definition (after artists created) or in the playback section. Playback section is better (it's about animation).\n\nThen `animate` returns `moving`, and FuncAnimation gets `blit=True`.\n\nEdits:\n1. In playback section, add `moving = ...` after `update(0)`/`start`.\n2. `animate` returns moving.\n3. FuncAnimation blit=True.\n\nCareful ordering: `update(0)` must run before blit background capture (FuncAnimation does its own draw). Fine.\n\nAlso the docstring mentions fixed scale; add a line about blitting? Maybe extend the module docstring: \"Only the artists that move are repainted (blit), so ~14 ms per frame instead of ~95 ms.\" Go