a: [ani._step() for _ in range(5)], 5))\\n\", \"path\": \"/tmp/check_blit.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "blit=True is in place; per-frame cost in my simulation is 13.8 ms (vs 95.7 baseline). But is the *actual* FuncAnimation blit path being exercised? My simulation manually reproduces it; let's verify with the real FuncAnimation blit machinery, i.e. drive `animation._step()` a few times and time it, with Agg. Also confirm the module's `animate` returns the list and mpl registers `_drawn_artists`.\n\nTest: after runpy with Agg + patched show, `ani = CAP[\"ani\"]`, `ani._drawn_artists` set? Check `ani._blit_cache` exists. Then time `ani._step()` N times (that's what the timer calls): `_step` → `_draw_next_frame` → `_pre_draw` (restore bg) → `func(...)` → `_post_draw` (draw_artist + blit).\n\nLet me write a check: time `ani._step()` median, and confirm `len(ani._drawn_artists) == 52`, and `ani._blit_cache` not None.\n\nAlso verify the animation still renders correctly: full `fig.canvas.draw()` to a PNG after some steps, compare pixel content vs non-blit baseline? The earlier PNG comparison is tricky. Simpler sanity: after N blit steps, save figure and check the panels have colored pixels (same as before). But blit draws on the canvas buffer; saving via `fig.savefig` does a full redraw (ignores blit) — so image check wouldn't validate blit output. To validate blit output, print the Agg buffer (`fig.canvas.buffer_rgba()`) after steps and check colored pixels there. The buffer contains the blitted result — good check that the artists were actually painted at the right places.\n\nAlso verify that the background is not stale-destroying earlier frames: with blit, restore_region(bg) → previous frame's artists erased. Test: draw frame A (mark position), then frame B; check buffer has no leftover from A. Practical check: run 2 steps with very different indices and compare buffer to a full-draw buffer of the same index. That's the real correctness check: buffer_after_blit(index) ≈ full