{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793178071.747654, "id": "bec8f0d6bea7", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_StQAbatblVaortgngFRW7644", "name": "write_file", "arguments": "{\"content\": \"import os, sys, runpy\\nimport matplotlib\\nmatplotlib.use(\\\"Agg\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nimport matplotlib.animation as manim\\n\\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\\nmanim.FuncAnimation = type(\\\"Cap\\\", (manim.FuncAnimation,), {})\\n\\ng = runpy.run_path(\\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\", run_name=\\\"__main__\\\")\\nfig, update, n = g[\\\"fig\\\"], g[\\\"update\\\"], g[\\\"N_FRAMES\\\"]\\nmoving = g[\\\"moving\\\"]\\n\\n\\ndef buf():\\n    return np.asarray(fig.canvas.buffer_rgba())[..., :3].astype(int)\\n\\n\\nupdate(0)\\nfig.canvas.draw()\\nbg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\\nprint(\\\"background captured, artists:\\\", len(moving))\\n\\nfor i in (0, 200, n - 1):\\n    update(i)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    full = buf()\\n    fig.canvas.restore_region(bg)\\n    for a in moving:\\n        a.axes.draw_artist(a)\\n    fig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\\n    blitted = buf()\\n    d = np.abs(blitted - full)\\n    update(i + 1 if i + 1 < n else i)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    neighbour = buf()\\n    dn = np.abs(neighbour - full)\\n    print(\\\"frame %4d: blit vs full %.3f%% differ (max %d) | full(i+1) vs full(i) %.3f%%\\\"\\n          % (i, 100.0 * (d.max(-1) > 8).mean(), d.max(),\\n             100.0 * (dn.max(-1) > 8).mean()))\\n\", \"path\": \"/tmp/check_blit2.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "`FuncAnimation._step()` = 12.3 ms per frame (vs 95.7 ms full draw) → 7.8× faster. \n\nBut the correctness check shows ~0.5-1% pixels differ with max diff 255 — meaning some pixels are wrong. Why? Possibly:\n1. The blitted frame is drawn at a different index than the reference: I set `start` so index ≈ i, but the wall clock advances between setting start and the step; for i=0, index could be 0 (fine). Let me check that the index matches: `glob[\"start\"] = time.perf_counter() - i*SECONDS/n` then `_step()` computes `index = int((now - start)/SECONDS*n)` = i + tiny → i (for i<n-1). For i = n-1, clamp → n-1. Should match.\n\n2. Ordering/zorder differences: blitting draws only the moving artists over background; the full draw draws panes/grid/axes then artists. Missing differences: maybe antialiasing over the axes background differs? No — background is restored identically.\n\n3. The real cause could be that `_step()` draws the *previous* frame's artists? `_step` → `_draw_next_frame(framedata, self._blit)` → `_pre_draw` restores bg, calls `func` (our animate → update(index)), then `_post_draw`. Hmm, but FuncAnimation blit has a twist: with blit=True, `_pre_draw` restores bg then calls `func`, and `_draw_frame`... let me look at mpl's `_draw_next_frame`:\n\n```python\n    def _draw_next_frame(self, framedata, blit):\n        # Breaks down the drawing of the next frame into steps of pre- and\n        # post- draw, while sending these events to the user's output.\n        self._pre_draw(framedata, blit)\n        self._draw_frame(framedata)\n        self._post_draw(framedata, blit)\n```\nSo `_pre_draw` (restore bg) happens BEFORE `_draw_frame` (which calls our animate → update data). Then `_post_draw` draws artists with `self._drawn_artists`. That's fine.\n\nWait — with blit, `_pre_draw` calls `self._fig.canvas.restore_region(self._blit_cache)` and then... also there's this known quirk:\n```python\n    def _pre_draw(self, framedata, blit):\n        # Fills the figure with Axes and artists, but no drawn artists.\n        self._fig.canvas.draw_idle()  # ??? \n```\nActually:\n```python\n    def _pre_draw(self, framedata, blit):\n        # Fills the figure with Axes and artists, but no drawn artists.\n        if blit:\n            # All the axes are already present in the background. But\n            # nothing is drawn yet, so we restore the background.\n            self._fig.canvas.restore_region(self._blit_cache)\n```\nThen `_post_draw`:\n```python\n    def _post_draw(self, framedata, blit):\n        if blit and self._drawn_artists:\n            self._blit_draw(self._drawn_artists)\n        else:\n            self._fig.canvas.draw_idle()\n```\n`_blit_draw` calls `ax.draw_artist(a)` for each artist, grouped by axes, then `self._fig.canvas.blit(self._fig.bbox)`.\n\nSo the mechanism is what I simulated. Then why the differences?\n\nHypothesis: my reference `fig.canvas.draw()` after `update(i)` — but I called `ani._step()` first, which had a background cached from an earlier state... the reference full draw should be authoritative. Differences of up to 255 in 0.5-1% pixels. Maybe due to *zorder*: an individual artist blit-drawn ignores zorder ordering and draws artists in list order, i.e. all 3D lines are drawn AFTER the panes/grid (background) — same as full draw. But our `moving` list order: world_frames lines, world texts, armor frames, plates, fills, edges, tags. In a full draw, order within an axes is by zorder then insertion. For 2D image axes: fills (patches, zorder=1), edges (lines zorder=2), tags (text zorder=3) — our list order matches (fills, edges, tags) → fine.\n\nFor the 3D axes: our `moving` order: triad lines (W, C, B), then labels W, C, B interleaved per triad: `for lines, text, _ in ...: for a in (*lines, text)` → W lines, W text, C lines, C text, B lines, B text. Full draw: children order... Axes3D `super().draw()` draws children sorted by zorder (all default zorder=2 for lines, 3 for text?) In mpl, `_AxesBase.draw` → `self._draw_children? ` it uses `martist.Artist._draw_list_compositing_images` over `self._children` sorted by zorder. Text default zorder=3, Line2D=2 → text drawn after all lines. In our blit order, W text is drawn before C and B lines. Overlap differences would only matter where text overlaps lines — the \"W\"/\"C\"/\"B\" labels sit at the frame origins, so they may overlap the lines! That could produce small pixel differences (0.5-1%).\n\nHmm, but that's a visual-quality matter: labels getting overdrawn by lines. Minor. But the 0.5-1% diff is suspiciously large for text overlap. Let me quantify where the diff pixels are — dump diff mask bounding boxes per panel. Alternatively check whether the difference is due to the *frame index mismatch* (e.g. index i vs i+1 differences would be large: plates moving by a few px → 0.5-1% of pixels differ! Indeed armor plates are ~30×20 px each and move; a 1-frame difference could easily be 0.5% pixels).\n\nTest: instead of using `start` offsets, directly call `update(i)` then simulate blit manually (restore bg, draw artists, blit) — no wall-clock involvement. Compare with full draw. Let me also compare against `update(i+1)` full draw to see which is closer (to detect off-by-one).\n\nActually simpler: in my manual simulation earlier (prof.py blit_frame), I used update with a counter, not comparing to reference. Let me write a precise comparison:\n\n```\nfor i in (...):\n    update(i); fig.canvas.draw(); full = buffer()\n    fig.canvas.restore_region(bg); [a.axes.draw_artist(a) for a in moving]; fig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\n```\nwait restore/draw/blit needs bg from a full draw of any frame; but then the buffer = bg + artists(i). Compare with full(i).\n\nBut careful: the full(i) draw sets M and everything; then bg was captured at some earlier frame — same M (view fixed) so bg identical. Then blit frame should equal full(i) exactly except ordering-of-artists effects.\n\nLet me test this properly, isolating from the wall clock. If the diff drops to ~0 (except text/line overlap), then the earlier diff was index mismatch and blit is correct.\n\nAlso test index mismatch hypothesis by comparing full(i) vs full(i+1) diff magnitude.\n\nLet me write /tmp/check_blit2.py."}
{"type": "tool_call", "timestamp": 1793178071.7476084, "id": "3c404f5b11ee", "name": "write_file", "arguments": "{\"content\": \"import os, sys, runpy\\nimport matplotlib\\nmatplotlib.use(\\\"Agg\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nimport matplotlib.animation as manim\\n\\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\\nmanim.FuncAnimation = type(\\\"Cap\\\", (manim.FuncAnimation,), {})\\n\\ng = runpy.run_path(\\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\", run_name=\\\"__main__\\\")\\nfig, update, n = g[\\\"fig\\\"], g[\\\"update\\\"], g[\\\"N_FRAMES\\\"]\\nmoving = g[\\\"moving\\\"]\\n\\n\\ndef buf():\\n    return np.asarray(fig.canvas.buffer_rgba())[..., :3].astype(int)\\n\\n\\nupdate(0)\\nfig.canvas.draw()\\nbg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\\nprint(\\\"background captured, artists:\\\", len(moving))\\n\\nfor i in (0, 200, n - 1):\\n    update(i)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    full = buf()\\n    fig.canvas.restore_region(bg)\\n    for a in moving:\\n        a.axes.draw_artist(a)\\n    fig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\\n    blitted = buf()\\n    d = np.abs(blitted - full)\\n    update(i + 1 if i + 1 < n else i)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    neighbour = buf()\\n    dn = np.abs(neighbour - full)\\n    print(\\\"frame %4d: blit vs full %.3f%% differ (max %d) | full(i+1) vs full(i) %.3f%%\\\"\\n          % (i, 100.0 * (d.max(-1) > 8).mean(), d.max(),\\n             100.0 * (dn.max(-1) > 8).mean()))\\n\", \"path\": \"/tmp/check_blit2.py\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_StQAbatblVaortgngFRW7644", "ui": {"group": null, "chip": "name", "summary": "", "preview": {"keys": ["✎path", "content"], "mode": "content"}, "header": "✓ wrote {path}", "form": "block", "body": "diff", "collapse": "long", "highlight": "", "chrome": "accent", "mutates": true, "undo": true}, "path_arg": "path"}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793178071.789737, "id": "02c2060d7eb3", "tool_call_id": "call_00_StQAbatblVaortgngFRW7644", "content": "OK: wrote /tmp/check_blit2.py (1184 chars)", "is_error": false, "diff": "--- a//tmp/check_blit2.py\n+++ b//tmp/check_blit2.py\n@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@\n+import os, sys, runpy\n+import matplotlib\n+matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\n+import numpy as np\n+import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n+import matplotlib.animation as manim\n+\n+sys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\n+plt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\n+manim.FuncAnimation = type(\"Cap\", (manim.FuncAnimation,), {})\n+\n+g = runpy.run_path(\"scripts/visualize_target.py\", run_name=\"__main__\")\n+fig, update, n = g[\"fig\"], g[\"update\"], g[\"N_FRAMES\"]\n+moving = g[\"moving\"]\n+\n+\n+def buf():\n+    return np.asarray(fig.canvas.buffer_rgba())[..., :3].astype(int)\n+\n+\n+update(0)\n+fig.canvas.draw()\n+bg = fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(fig.bbox)\n+print(\"background captured, artists:\", len(moving))\n+\n+for i in (0, 200, n - 1):\n+    update(i)\n+    fig.canvas.draw()\n+    full = buf()\n+    fig.canvas.restore_region(bg)\n+    for a in moving:\n+        a.axes.draw_artist(a)\n+    fig.canvas.blit(fig.bbox)\n+    blitted = buf()\n+    d = np.abs(blitted - full)\n+    update(i + 1 if i + 1 < n else i)\n+    fig.canvas.draw()\n+    neighbour = buf()\n+    dn = np.abs(neighbour - full)\n+    print(\"frame %4d: blit vs full %.3f%% differ (max %d) | full(i+1) vs full(i) %.3f%%\"\n+          % (i, 100.0 * (d.max(-1) > 8).mean(), d.max(),\n+             100.0 * (dn.max(-1) > 8).mean()))\n"}
