copies with a different alpha handling (premultiplied?) → tiny color differences but capped to 255? Max diff 255 is large though.\n\nLet me look at actual numbers in the tag region: print blitted vs full pixel values where they differ, e.g. a sample.\n\nActually — hold on. A simpler explanation: **the tags' `set_position` uses `u[m].mean(), v[m].mean()`, and the text is drawn with `clip_on` default True, clipped to the axes bbox**... irrelevant.\n\nEven simpler: in the full draw, the text tag positions are recomputed by `update(i)`; I then call `fig.canvas.draw()` → uses same positions. In the blit path, `restore_region(bg)` then `draw_artist(a)` for each; for text, `ax.draw_artist(text)` sets `text.set_clip_box/...` — same.\n\nHmm, what if the difference is caused by the *number of text rasterization caches*: mpl caches rasterized text glyphs per (font, size, angle, color) — identical.\n\nOK, empirical: sample the differing pixels and print values, plus check if the differences form a \"ghost\" of a *previous* frame (i.e. stale leftovers) — that would indicate the background restore didn't cover the artists because bg was captured when artists were at frame 0 and the restored background contains... wait! The background was captured at frame 0 **with the frame-0 artists already drawn on it** — `fig.canvas.draw()` draws EVERYTHING including the artists at frame 0! Then `copy_from_bbox` caches a background that has the frame-0 artists baked in. So blitted frames show ghosts of frame 0 underneath!\n\nThat's the bug: standard blit procedure requires capturing the background *without* the moving artists — mpl's own `_blit_draw`/`_post_draw` for FuncAnimation does exactly:\n- `_pre_draw`: `if blit: self._fig.canvas.restore_region(self._blit_cache)`, and the background is captured in `_setup_blit` after **hiding** the drawn artists? Let me recall mpl's `_setup_blit`:\n\n```python\n    def _setup_blit(self):\n        # Setting up the blit requires: a cache of the background