rs only the artists given instead\n        # of the entire figure.\n        updated_ax = {a.axes for a in artists}\n        # Enumerate artists to cache Axes backgrounds. We do not draw\n        # artists yet to not cache foreground from plots with shared Axes\n        for ax in updated_ax:\n            # If we haven't cached the background for this Axes, add it\n            if ax not in self._blit_cache:\n                ax.fig.canvas.draw_idle()\n                self._blit_cache[ax] = ax.fig.canvas.copy_from_bbox(ax.bbox)? \n```\nNo... Let me just read the installed source. The key point: mpl sets the moving artists to `animated=True` while capturing the background! That's the standard trick: `artist.set_animated(True)` → artists with animated=True are skipped during normal draw → background captured without them. In FuncAnimation: `self._drawn_artists = []` and artists returned by func get `set_animated(True)`? Let me check the source for \"animated\".\n\nSo my simulation was flawed (bg contained frame-0 artists) — but the real `FuncAnimation` path sets `animated=True` on the returned artists, so the background is clean. However! With blit=True, mpl draws the artists via `ax.draw_artist()`, which works for animated artists.\n\nBUT: does `_blit_draw` set animated? Let me check the source. If mpl relies on the user not needing it... Let me read `animation.py` around `_setup_blit`/`_blit_draw`.\n\nCrucially, the earlier /tmp/check_blit.py test used the real `ani._step()` and found ~0.5-1% diffs vs full draw. If mpl properly captures a clean background, diffs should be tiny. The diffs were similar in size to my manual test (0.52%). Hmm, both ~0.5%, consistent.\n\nSo there IS a real discrepancy in the real path too. Cause? Possibly the same ghost issue: mpl's `_pre_draw` with blit does `self._fig.canvas.restore_region(self._blit_cache)` where `_blit_cache` is a **dict of axes → region**? Let me read the code to understand exactly, then diagnose.\n\nLet me read animati