{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793178225.741311, "id": "ce81c15ae304", "content": "差异出现在两个方向上，这说明我的测试脚本有问题：`runpy` 返回的是模块全局变量的*副本*，所以 `animate` 里的 `start` 实际上从未被重置。我来正确修正测试脚本。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_GvUZ8S3zZhdCYRClb5257971", "name": "write_file", "arguments": "{\"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Blit vs full draw, with the frame index pinned through the real globals.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport os\\nimport runpy\\nimport sys\\nimport time\\n\\nimport matplotlib\\nmatplotlib.use(\\\"Agg\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\\n\\ng = runpy.run_path(\\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\", run_name=\\\"__main__\\\")\\nfig, moving, ani = g[\\\"fig\\\"], g[\\\"moving\\\"], g[\\\"animation\\\"]\\nn, seconds = g[\\\"N_FRAMES\\\"], g[\\\"SECONDS\\\"]\\nfns = g[\\\"animate\\\"].__globals__          # the module's real namespace\\nseen = []\\n\\n\\ndef spy(index):\\n    seen.append(index)\\n    g[\\\"update\\\"](index)\\n\\n\\nfns[\\\"update\\\"] = spy                     # animate() looks update up in globals\\n\\n\\ndef buf():\\n    return np.asarray(fig.canvas.buffer_rgba())[..., :3].astype(int)\\n\\n\\ndef snap(i):\\n    g[\\\"update\\\"](i)\\n    saved = [a.get_animated() for a in moving]\\n    for a in moving:\\n        a.set_animated(False)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    out = buf()\\n    for a, was in zip(moving, saved):\\n        a.set_animated(was)\\n    return out\\n\\n\\ndef step(i):\\n    fns[\\\"start\\\"] = time.perf_counter() - (i + 0.5) * seconds / n\\n    seen.clear()\\n    ani._step()\\n    return buf(), seen[-1]\\n\\n\\ndef report(tag, i):\\n    img, idx = step(i)\\n    ref = snap(i)\\n    d = np.abs(img - ref).max(-1)\\n    print(\\\"%-9s asked %3d -> drew %3d: %5d px differ (max %3d)\\\"\\n          % (tag, i, idx, (d > 8).sum(), d.max()))\\n\\n\\nfig.canvas.draw()                       # what plt.show() does before ticking\\nfor i in (0, 120, 300, 599):\\n    report(\\\"dirty bg\\\", i)\\n\\nfor a in moving:                        # clean background, mpl cache dropped\\n    a.set_animated(True)\\nfig.canvas.draw()\\nani._blit_cache.clear()\\nfor i in (0, 120, 300, 599):\\n    report(\\\"clean bg\\\", i)\\n\", \"path\": \"/tmp/check_blit5.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The differences are at line/text pixels with completely different content: e.g. blit shows green where full shows axes background (242 = 3D pane). So the two images have *different content* — it looks like a shift, i.e., the blit frame's artists are at positions of a *different* frame index than full's frame 0!\n\nWait, in this test, `step(i)` calls `ani._step()` which calls animate → update(index) using wall clock. I set g[\"start\"] so index = i. Then `snap(i)` calls update(i) again → same i=0. So contents should be identical... unless `ani._step()` at index 0 does something else.\n\nUnless the wall clock start math is off and `ani._step()` rendered index != 0. Let me print the index that animate computes. Add a check: after step, compare against snap(j) for several j to find which index matches best. Actually easier: read the code: `index = min(int((time.perf_counter() - start) / SECONDS * N_FRAMES), N_FRAMES - 1)`; with start = now - (i+0.5)*seconds/n → (pc-start) ≈ (i+0.5)*seconds/n → index ≈ (i+0.5) → int → i. Correct (if pc increases a bit after setting start, index could round to i+... small; e.g. pc advances by 1 ms → 1ms/(10/600)=0.06 → index i still).\n\nHmm, but magnitudes: the differences look like a *shift* of content. In 3D panels, blit has green where full has background — suggests the blit frame has extra lines (i.e., ghost of another frame overlay). And full has color where blit has background, e.g. (882,241) full = blue-ish [67 140 190]?? That's a weird blue - probably AA blend. Hmm, full has content where blit doesn't.\n\nWait — could it be that in `snap()` the full draw *includes* the animated artists (we set them False) plus something else... and `step()` at frame 0 doesn't include some artists? At frame 0 they'd be same.\n\nAhh, I bet the issue: `ani._step()` with `_blit_cache` from the initial `fig.canvas.draw()` — the cache is taken during the *first* `_blit_draw` from the canvas buffer at that time. That buffer is from the initial draw which had update(0) applied but artists set animated only *during* `_init_draw`, i.e., after the draw. So the initial buffer HAS frame-0 artists. But that's the same content as frame 0! And the first `_step` at index 0: `_pre_draw` → `_blit_clear` cache empty → nothing; `_draw_frame` → update(0); `_post_draw` → `_blit_draw` → cache miss → bg = copy_from_bbox (dirty with frame 0, but frame 0 is current anyway) → draw_artist each → blit. So the resulting image = frame0 background-with-artists + artists drawn again on top. For opaque lines, doubling is invisible; for alpha fills (0.35), drawing twice darkens! And for text, AA doubling changes.\n\nBut diff at frame 0 shows blit missing content where full has it. Hmm, e.g. (882,241): full has [67 140 190] light blue, blit [242 242 242] (pane). That means full has something blit lacks. And (880,276): full red [213 45 46], blit [193 193 193] grey. Grey = the world_plates color #666666 at lw1 with AA? Or \"BODY\" text? Hmm.\n\nSo both directions of difference → different positions. So the two frames rendered are genuinely different index/content.\n\nUnless... hmm! `snap()` uses `fig.canvas.draw()` which draws animated=False artists, BUT `moving` includes `Text` objects (tags) whose position was set... same.\n\nWait wait. Maybe the difference is not content but *canvas size* / dpi? No.\n\nLet me reconsider: could the blit frame be *frame 0* while snap is also frame 0, but the artists' data got mutated between (update called again, same data)? No.\n\nHmm, but consider `place()` with `origin + length * rot.T` — deterministic.\n\nAlternatively: maybe `step()` didn't render index 0 but some other index because `g[\"start\"]` assignment doesn't affect the function's globals? runpy's returned dict IS the module's globals for run_path? For `runpy.run_path`, the returned dict is the module namespace dict; functions defined there have `__globals__` equal to that dict. Actually the docs say the returned dict is a \"module globals dictionary\" — I believe `__globals__` is the same object. But not 100% sure: run_path creates a new globals dict, executes code there, returns a *copy*? Docs: \"run_path ... returns the resulting module globals dictionary.\" I recall `run_module`/`run_path` return a copy for `run_module`? Hmm, `runpy.run_module` docstring: \"Return the resulting module globals dictionary.\" And `_run_module_code` returns `mod_globals.copy()`. Yes! In runpy's implementation: \n\n```python\ndef _run_module_code(code, init_globals=None, mod_name=None, ...):\n    ...\n    try:\n        exec(code, mod_globals)\n    finally:\n        ...\n    return mod_globals.copy()\n```\n\nYes, runpy copies. So `g[\"start\"] = ...` does NOT change the module's actual `start` global! So `animate` uses the original `start` from import time, meaning index = large (seconds since start). At frame 0 test, index could be ~ (elapsed)/10*600 → some middle frame. That explains everything: blit frame index ≠ 0.\n\nSo my test harness is broken, but the earlier finding (dirty vs clean bg identical diffs) is also suspect... Well, if index differs from what snap(i) draws, diffs are expected regardless of bg. Need to fix the harness: patch `time.perf_counter` in the module's globals? Since animate's globals is the real module dict, and runpy gave us a copy — but the functions' `__globals__` point to the real dict. We can do `animate.__globals__[\"start\"] = ...`. \n\nAlso `g[\"moving\"]` etc. are fine (objects shared).\n\nLet me redo the test using `ani._func.__globals__[\"start\"] = ...` to set the clock.\n\nAlso, alternatively, monkeypatch `g[\"animate\"]`? Simpler: set `start` via `ani._func.__globals__`. But careful: in the real script, `animate` reads module global `start`. Set `fns = g[\"animate\"].__globals__; fns[\"start\"] = ...`. Since `g[\"animate\"]` is the function object, `__globals__` is the true module namespace. Good.\n\nLet me fix the harness in both check_blit4 and check_blit5. I'll rewrite check_blit5 to (a) set clock properly, (b) verify which index was actually rendered (by comparing blit to full redraws for a few candidate indices), then report diff.\n\nAlso: to be safe, verify the rendered index by instrumenting: wrap `update`? `update` is a module global too, used by `animate` via globals → I can wrap: `fns[\"update\"] = wrapper` which records index. That's clean and gives ground truth.\n\nLet's do that."}
{"type": "tool_call", "timestamp": 1793178225.7412577, "id": "024a8a557eca", "name": "write_file", "arguments": "{\"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Blit vs full draw, with the frame index pinned through the real globals.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport os\\nimport runpy\\nimport sys\\nimport time\\n\\nimport matplotlib\\nmatplotlib.use(\\\"Agg\\\")\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\nsys.path.insert(0, os.getcwd())\\nplt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\\n\\ng = runpy.run_path(\\\"scripts/visualize_target.py\\\", run_name=\\\"__main__\\\")\\nfig, moving, ani = g[\\\"fig\\\"], g[\\\"moving\\\"], g[\\\"animation\\\"]\\nn, seconds = g[\\\"N_FRAMES\\\"], g[\\\"SECONDS\\\"]\\nfns = g[\\\"animate\\\"].__globals__          # the module's real namespace\\nseen = []\\n\\n\\ndef spy(index):\\n    seen.append(index)\\n    g[\\\"update\\\"](index)\\n\\n\\nfns[\\\"update\\\"] = spy                     # animate() looks update up in globals\\n\\n\\ndef buf():\\n    return np.asarray(fig.canvas.buffer_rgba())[..., :3].astype(int)\\n\\n\\ndef snap(i):\\n    g[\\\"update\\\"](i)\\n    saved = [a.get_animated() for a in moving]\\n    for a in moving:\\n        a.set_animated(False)\\n    fig.canvas.draw()\\n    out = buf()\\n    for a, was in zip(moving, saved):\\n        a.set_animated(was)\\n    return out\\n\\n\\ndef step(i):\\n    fns[\\\"start\\\"] = time.perf_counter() - (i + 0.5) * seconds / n\\n    seen.clear()\\n    ani._step()\\n    return buf(), seen[-1]\\n\\n\\ndef report(tag, i):\\n    img, idx = step(i)\\n    ref = snap(i)\\n    d = np.abs(img - ref).max(-1)\\n    print(\\\"%-9s asked %3d -> drew %3d: %5d px differ (max %3d)\\\"\\n          % (tag, i, idx, (d > 8).sum(), d.max()))\\n\\n\\nfig.canvas.draw()                       # what plt.show() does before ticking\\nfor i in (0, 120, 300, 599):\\n    report(\\\"dirty bg\\\", i)\\n\\nfor a in moving:                        # clean background, mpl cache dropped\\n    a.set_animated(True)\\nfig.canvas.draw()\\nani._blit_cache.clear()\\nfor i in (0, 120, 300, 599):\\n    report(\\\"clean bg\\\", i)\\n\", \"path\": \"/tmp/check_blit5.py\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_GvUZ8S3zZhdCYRClb5257971", "ui": {"group": null, "chip": "name", "summary": "", "preview": {"keys": ["✎path", "content"], "mode": "content"}, "header": "✓ wrote {path}", "form": "block", "body": "diff", "collapse": "long", "highlight": "", "chrome": "accent", "mutates": true, "undo": true}, "path_arg": "path"}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793178225.7845547, "id": "d77f555a2b18", "tool_call_id": "call_00_GvUZ8S3zZhdCYRClb5257971", "content": "OK: wrote /tmp/check_blit5.py (+34 -26 lines)", "is_error": false, "diff": "--- a//tmp/check_blit5.py\n+++ b//tmp/check_blit5.py\n@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@\n-\"\"\"Where exactly do the blitted and fully drawn frames disagree?\"\"\"\n+\"\"\"Blit vs full draw, with the frame index pinned through the real globals.\"\"\"\n import os\n import runpy\n import sys\n@@ -13,9 +13,18 @@\n plt.show = lambda *a, **k: None\n \n g = runpy.run_path(\"scripts/visualize_target.py\", run_name=\"__main__\")\n-fig, update = g[\"fig\"], g[\"update\"]\n-n, moving, ani = g[\"N_FRAMES\"], g[\"moving\"], g[\"animation\"]\n-seconds, axes = g[\"SECONDS\"], (g[\"ax_world\"], g[\"ax_armor\"], g[\"ax_image\"])\n+fig, moving, ani = g[\"fig\"], g[\"moving\"], g[\"animation\"]\n+n, seconds = g[\"N_FRAMES\"], g[\"SECONDS\"]\n+fns = g[\"animate\"].__globals__          # the module's real namespace\n+seen = []\n+\n+\n+def spy(index):\n+    seen.append(index)\n+    g[\"update\"](index)\n+\n+\n+fns[\"update\"] = spy                     # animate() looks update up in globals\n \n \n def buf():\n@@ -23,7 +32,7 @@\n \n \n def snap(i):\n-    update(i)\n+    g[\"update\"](i)\n     saved = [a.get_animated() for a in moving]\n     for a in moving:\n         a.set_animated(False)\n@@ -35,28 +44,27 @@\n \n \n def step(i):\n-    g[\"start\"] = time.perf_counter() - (i + 0.5) * seconds / n\n+    fns[\"start\"] = time.perf_counter() - (i + 0.5) * seconds / n\n+    seen.clear()\n     ani._step()\n-    return buf()\n+    return buf(), seen[-1]\n \n \n+def report(tag, i):\n+    img, idx = step(i)\n+    ref = snap(i)\n+    d = np.abs(img - ref).max(-1)\n+    print(\"%-9s asked %3d -> drew %3d: %5d px differ (max %3d)\"\n+          % (tag, i, idx, (d > 8).sum(), d.max()))\n+\n+\n+fig.canvas.draw()                       # what plt.show() does before ticking\n+for i in (0, 120, 300, 599):\n+    report(\"dirty bg\", i)\n+\n+for a in moving:                        # clean background, mpl cache dropped\n+    a.set_animated(True)\n fig.canvas.draw()\n-i = 0\n-blit, full = step(i), snap(i)\n-d = np.abs(blit - full).max(-1)\n-bad = d > 8\n-ys, xs = np.where(bad)\n-print(\"frame %d: %d px differ, x %d..%d, y %d..%d\"\n-      % (i, bad.sum(), xs.min(), xs.max(), ys.min(), ys.max()))\n-for name, ax in zip((\"world\", \"armor\", \"image\"), axes):\n-    b = ax.bbox\n-    m = bad[int(b.y0):int(b.y1), int(b.x0):int(b.x1)]\n-    print(\"  %-5s bbox x %4d..%4d y %4d..%4d  %5d px differ (%.2f%% of axes)\"\n-          % (name, b.x0, b.x1, b.y0, b.y1, m.sum(),\n-             100.0 * m.mean()))\n-cols = np.bincount(xs)\n-print(\"columns with hits:\", np.where(cols)[0].min(), np.where(cols)[0].max(),\n-      \"distinct columns:\", (cols > 0).sum())\n-for k in range(0, len(ys), max(1, len(ys) // 8)):\n-    y, x = ys[k], xs[k]\n-    print(\"  (%4d,%4d) full=%s blit=%s\" % (x, y, full[y, x], blit[y, x]))\n+ani._blit_cache.clear()\n+for i in (0, 120, 300, 599):\n+    report(\"clean bg\", i)\n"}
