eported same 17 px as clean. So the background cache must actually be clean.\n\nWhy? Because `copy_from_bbox` on the *Agg* canvas returns from the buffer... At the time of `_blit_draw` first call, my harness had already done `fig.canvas.draw()` at a moment when artists were... wait no: in the \"dirty\" case, `fig.canvas.draw()` was called while artists were animated=False (default False at import? They were set animated during `_init_draw` → `_draw_frame` → `animate(0)` → sets animated True). Hmm, the sequence: import sets `moving` but animated flags default False. Then the `fig.canvas.draw()` in my harness triggers draw_event → `_start` → `_init_draw` → `_draw_frame(0)` → animate(0) → `set_animated(True)`. But that full draw already drew the frame-0 artists into the buffer (flags were False during that draw). So buffer = frame 0 with artists. Then first `_step` at index 0: cache miss → bg = buffer containing frame 0 artists → then draws artists again → would double-draw the alpha fills and AA text at index 0.\n\nBut the comparison of blit vs full at index 0 shows only 11 px differing. So the fills aren't doubled → background must be clean. Why? Maybe `copy_from_bbox` on CanvasAgg returns... `copy_from_bbox(bbox)` → `self.buffer_rgba()` slice copy → clean? It includes artists if drawn.\n\nHmm, unless the initial draw in `_start` doesn't include them because... at `plt.show()` time in real use, the figure is drawn once by the backend before draw_event handlers? The 3D artists would be drawn with animated=False at that point.\n\nHmm, but maybe `fig.canvas.draw()` (my harness) → `draw_event` fired *after* rendering, and `_init_draw` sets animated True; the buffer retains frame-0 artists. Then `_blit_draw` caches the buffer... unless `Axes3D`/`Axes.draw` track... Let me test directly: measure the cached bg content. Actually easier: after the first `_step`, compare blit composite vs full draw: 11 px differ → so no doubling. Therefore the bg is clean. So maybe mpl's initial `_start`/`_init_draw` sequence re-draws the figure: In `_start`, `mpl_disconnect` then `self._init_draw()`. FuncAnimation._init_draw... calls `self._draw_frame(frame_data)`. Hmm, but where's the \"clear the initial frame\" full draw? Comment at line 1129: `anim._init_draw()  # Clear the initial frame`. Let me look at lines 1100–1140 to see if there's a draw. Also `Animation._init_draw` in base sets `_draw_was_started`; base `Animation.__init__` may call `self._fig.canvas.draw_idle()`.\n\nWhatever — empirically clean. Good: no ghost.\n\n11-19 px differ with max 14 — negligible AA/order difference. Fine.\n\nSo the blit change is correct. Now let me re-verify the whole thing once more: run the headless validation script (/tmp/check_simple.py) to ensure artist counts, limits, animate behavior still fine — nothing changed in the file since. Actually the file hasn't changed since that verification; only tests changed. So no need.\n\nBut wait: is there still a real concern about the initial draw? Since the ghost question is settled empirically (no ghost), fine.\n\nAlso should I double check the real TkAgg run? Summary says done (2 s version). Fine.\n\nOne remaining question: is `blit=True` with `moving` 52 artists correct w.r.t. zorder sorting? Yes verified.\n\nHmm, but one real risk with blit: on window resize, `ax._get_view()` changes → background re-cached → still fine (but artists positions in pixels change; they're re-drawn). Good.\n\nNow, about `set_visible(front[m])` for fills: The `fill` artists are animated, so ok.\n\nSo, everything is verified. The task \"改\" is complete: blit applied, performance 95.7 → 12.3 ms/frame, correctness verified to 11–19 px AA-level difference.\n\nShould I make any further change? The user said \"改\" as the final instruction, and I already made the change. The remaining step is to report. But maybe I should consider whether to also drop `set_axis_off` idea — no, not adopted.\n\nLet me do a final sanity check: run the real TkAgg vers