{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793178251.7038138, "id": "fbdcc8cb7e3a", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_7JaqLwRejWMCMxhrL5w90172", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && .venv/bin/python /tmp/check_simple.py 2>&1 | tail -15\"}"}], "reasoning": "Real Tk: 17.1 ms median vs 101 ms full draw → 5.9×, and 17.1 ms < 16.7 ms (60 fps) barely — slightly over one frame budget. Since the animation uses wall-clock frame skipping, a 17 ms frame just drops some. Could we shave? Options:\n\n- Reduce dpi (fig dpi default 100, figure 16.5x5.2 in = 1650x520). Lower dpi to 90 → fewer pixels to blit. Summary says dpi 60/80/100 didn't change full-draw time (because text/3D path dominates?), but blit cost scales with pixel area partially. Actually for blit, the cost is: restore region (numpy copy) + draw 52 artists + 3 image puts. Drawing artists is vector work independent of dpi mostly; the blits copy pixel data.\n\n- `fig.set_dpi`? Not worth it; window size shrinks.\n\n17 ms is fine — animation runs at ~58 fps with occasional drops. Good enough. The user asked for lower latency; 101 → 17 ms is 5.9×.\n\nNow: is there anything else I should change on the code? Task \"改\" is done. I'll report concisely in Chinese.\n\nBefore reporting, run the earlier full headless verification once to confirm the file still passes (nothing changed, but cheap): /tmp/check_simple.py."}
