ke `rotate_deg` with huge value) it wouldn't matter (angle mod 360)... but if the *o*blique/skew...\n\nHmm, what about `text` inside an axes with a **huge aspect/log scale**? no.\n\nI think the most likely culprit for THIS script: an axes with `set_box_aspect` and a *degenerate* 3D projection (e.g., all points collinear → `get_proj` produces inf/NaN) → `Text3D.draw` → `proj_transform` → NaN → `proj3d` → then `Text.draw` at NaN with a transform... → `draw_text` with NaN coordinates → glyph raster at NaN → raster overflow? Possibly.\n\nHmm, but 0x62 raster overflow specifically means the raster buffer can't hold the glyph outline's bbox — happens when the outline bbox is huge in 26.6 units.\n\nLet me look at the code. Perhaps matplotlib's `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` calls `font._render_glyph(g, ...)` where the font was configured with `set_size` using `fontsize * dpi/72` — no transform. So the only way to make it huge is a huge fontsize.\n\nAH WAIT. I bet I know: **`figsize=(16.5, 5.2)` and `dpi` default 100** — no.\n\nHmm, is there any text with a *computed* fontsize? `fig.suptitle(..., fontsize=9)`, titles default 12, `ax_image.set_title(f\"camera view at {DISTANCE:.1f} m ...\")` — a *long* string! `IMAGE_W}x{IMAGE_H}` with 1280x1024. Long title → wide, but glyph size fine.\n\nCould the raster overflow be caused by **too many glyphs in the raster pool at once**? Raster_Overflow is per-glyph.\n\nLet me look up: matplotlib issue \"raster overflow\" — I recall matplotlib 3.10/3.9 has `FT_Render_Glyph failed with error 0x62: raster overflow` when rendering text with a **very large font size relative to the figure dpi**, e.g., `fontsize=1e4`. Also reported when using `ft2font` with **hinting** and a *rotation* of 90°? Hmm.\n\nGiven uncertainty, best: search the web for the exact error string with matplotlib. Let me do that (web_search available). Then read backend_agg source to see how size is computed. Both cheap.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider the *specific* context: the earlier conversation established that this error occurred at the very start (before blit existed?). The first message in this conversation has the same traceback including `_blit_draw`. So the script at that time had blit. And the user asked \"这是怎么回事\". Then the summary says the work was: simplify + blit. So the crash was reported at the start, and it was NOT resolved?! The summary's \"Next Move\" was about pixel diff, not this crash. Hmm, the crash at the top: `_post_draw` → `_blit_draw` → `a.axes.draw_artist(a)` → Text draw → raster overflow.\n\nNow the user asks again with the same traceback, likely because it still happens (or happens again after they added the v/a lines).\n\nKey hypothesis: with the target moving far away (or the axes having huge extents), the **3D text** (triad labels \"W\"/\"C\"/\"B\"/\"A1\"...) gets projected to a huge pixel coordinate, and `Text3D.draw` calls `super().draw()`… and in `Text.draw`, there's `self._get_layout(renderer)` which calls `renderer.get_text_width_height_descent(s, prop, ismath)` — that's for the *whole* text, size from fontsize.\n\nBUT: there's also `TextPath`? no.\n\nHmm, `Text.draw` at line 909 in this mpl version: `textrenderer.draw_text(gc, x, y, clean_line, self._fontproperties, angle, ismath=ismath, mtext=self)`. So `draw_text` gets `angle`. Then backend_agg's `draw_text`:\n\n```python\ndef draw_text(self, gc, x, y, s, prop, angle, ismath=False, mtext=None):\n    ...\n    font = self._prepare_font(prop)\n    font.set_text(s, angle)\n    font.draw_glyphs_to_bitmap(antialiased=...)\n    ...\n```\nand `font.set_text(s, angle)` — in ft2font, `set_text` takes the angle and computes `FT_Matrix` for rotation... and then `draw_glyphs_to_bitmap` handles the actual rasterization; `_render_glyph` is called lazily per glyph in newer mpl (3.10+ has `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes`).\n\nIn `ft2font_wrapper`, `_render_glyph(g, ...)` calls `FT_Load_Glyph` with flags and then `FT_Render_Glyph`. The size is set by `set_size(fontsize, dpi)`.\n\nSo a huge `fontsize` is needed... OR the *dpi* is huge. Is the renderer's dpi huge? `canvas.get_renderer()` → `CanvasAgg.__init__` sets `self.renderer = RendererAgg(w, h, self.figure.dpi)`. Figure dpi = rcParams['figure.dpi'] = 100 (or the backend may apply screen dpi for TkAgg? `FigureCanvasTkAgg` uses figure dpi). Not huge.\n\nSo how does this error arise here? Maybe it's not about size at all: FT_Err_Raster_Overflow (0x62) can also occur when the glyph's **outline has enormous coordinates** — e.g., a *composite* glyph or a glyph with a broken transform due to the rotation matrix. If the angle is NaN, `FT_Set_Transform` gets NaNs → the rasterizer's bbox becomes garbage → raster overflow. That's plausible!\n\nWhere could NaN come from? `Text3D`: `angle` for Text3D... `Text.draw` computes `angle = self.get_rotation()`, and if `transform_rotates_text` is set, it derives the angle from the transform — for Text3D, `transform_rotates_text` might be... hmm.\n\nAlternatively, NaN position: `x, y` NaN → `draw_text(gc, nan, nan, ...)`. In `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes`, positions are computed as floats; NaN position wouldn't cause raster overflow (just no output).\n\nOK let me stop speculating and get data: (1) read backend_agg.py 150-260; (2) search web for the error. Let me do both in parallel.\n\nActually, another important thought: this error is *known* to be triggered by **very large glyph rotation matrices when `Text` uses a transform with extreme scale** — specifically, matplotlib's `Text` with `transform_rotates_text` computes an angle from the transform; if `ax.datalim`-scale is 1e-9 that's fine.\n\nHmm, but hold on. What if the issue is a **huge 3D projected coordinate** (like 1e6) leading to a huge `angle`? no.\n\nWhat about `fontsize` being a **numpy array**? no.\n\nLet me also consider that the error might come from rendering a *space* or the `A#` text when the *axes* has become degenerate: `ax_image` with `xlim` reversed and infinite span? If `half` becomes inf/NaN (e.g., u_all contains inf because a corner has cam_z ≈ 0 — i.e., the target passes *through* the camera plane!), then `set_xlim(nan, nan)` → the axes transform becomes NaN → but glyph size is unaffected...\n\nHmm, unless: `Text` inside an axes with a NaN transform → `renderer.points_to_pixels` fine...\n\nOK here's another possibility I should take seriously: matplotlib's `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` computes, for each glyph, `bitmap = font._render_glyph(g, flags, ...)` — and ft2font's `_render_glyph` sets the face's transform from the *stored* matrix. In mpl ≥3.9, `_render_glyph` signature includes `(gindex, hinting_factor, *, _kerning_factor=0)`. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the actual code and then search. Data first.\n\nLet me read backend_agg.py lines 150-260 and ft2font wrapper's _render_glyph if accessible (compiled .so, but .pyi exists).\n\nThen web search.\n\nPlan: 3 tool calls in parallel:\n1. read backend_agg.py 140-260\n2. grep ft2font .pyi for _render_glyph\n3. web_search \"FT_Render_Glyph raster overflow matplotlib 0x62\"\n\nThen reason about the script's specific trigger: I suspect the *huge* projected coordinates of `Text3D` in a 3D axes when limits are large... but before that, let me consider: does `Text3D.draw` call `self.set_fontsize`? No.\n\nHmm! Actually WAIT. I recall something specific: matplotlib's `Text3D` uses `self._x, self._y, self._z` and `draw()` does:\n\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        position3d = np.atleast_2d(np.asarray(self._position3d))\n        proj = proj3d.proj_transform(position3d[..., 0], position3d[..., 1],\n                                     position3d[..., 2], self.axes.M)\n        self.set_position((proj[0][0], proj[1][0]))\n        super().draw(renderer)\n```\nAnd `Text.draw` uses `self.get_transform()` = ... For Text3D created via `ax.text(x,y,z,s)`, `art3d.text_2d_to_3d` sets `self._transform = IdentityTransform()`? Let me check: in `text_2d_to_3d`:\n```python\ndef text_2d_to_3d(obj, z=0, zdir='z'):\n    ...\n    obj._position3d = (x, y, z)\n    # ...\n    obj.set_transform(IdentityTransform())  # ? \n```\nHmm, actually I recall it sets `obj._transform = IdentityTransform()` and `obj.set_position((x,y))`? Not sure. If the transform remains the *data transform* of the axes, then for a 3D axes the 2D data transform is applied to the 3D-projected coords → wildly wrong but not a crash.\n\nOK, data. Let me also reconsider the most mundane explanation: **the crash is a known matplotlib/FreeType bug triggered by `fontsize` combined with the `Text` being *huge* because `ax.text` inside a 3D axes inherits `fontsize` 8 — no.\n\nAlternatively: the error is triggered by *some* glyph at *rotation* — e.g. `Text3D` with `angle` derived from `zdir`! YES: `ax.text(x, y, z, s, zdir='z')` and `text_2d_to_3d` sets an `angle` for the 3D text? Hmm, `Text3D` does set `self._rotation`? There's `art3d.text_2d_to_3d(obj, z=0, zdir)`:\n\n```python\ndef text_2d_to_3d(obj, z=0, zdir='z'):\n    \"\"\"Convert a Text to a Text3D object.\"\"\"\n    obj._z = z\n    obj._zdir = zdir\n    obj._verticalalignment = 'bottom'\n    obj._horizontalalignment = 'left'\n    obj.__class__ = Text3D\n```\nand `Text3D.draw`:\n```python\n    def draw(self, renderer):\n        position3d = np.atleast_2d(np.asarray(self._position3d))\n        proj = proj3d.proj_transform(position3d[..., 0], position3d[..., 1],\n                                     position3d[..., 2], self.axes.M)\n        self.set_position((proj[0][0], proj[1][0]))\n        super().draw(renderer)\n```\nNo rotation. OK.\n\nEnough. Let me gather data.\n\nActually — hmm, one more strong hypothesis: **`fontsize` from `rcParams` is fine, but the *Tk* backend's renderer dpi can be the screen dpi (e.g., 96) — fine.\n\nAnd Raster_Overflow with normal-size glyphs happens in FreeType when the *raster pool* (gray raster) can't be allocated... but 0x62 is specifically \"raster overflow\" = outline too big (more than `SHRT_MAX` 26.6 fixed-point = 32767/64 ≈ 512 px? no, it's about the number of spans). FreeType's `ft_raster1_render` has `FT_Err_Raster_Overflow` when \"the outline or bitmap is too large\" (e.g., > 2^15 pixels in some dimension). Hmm, 11 px glyph can't.\n\nBUT: if the FT_Face's size is set with a *huge* `dpi` (e.g., 1e9) the glyph becomes huge. Where would a huge dpi come from? `RendererAgg(w, h, dpi)` — `figure.dpi`. In the Tk backend, `FigureCanvasTkAgg` uses `figure.dpi` too, but `_backend_tk` sets `self.figure.dpi` from the Tk `tk scaling`?? Hmm! There IS something: `FigureManagerTk` / `FigureCanvasTkAgg.__init__` → `self._tkcanvas ... ` and in `_backend_tk.py`, `FigureCanvasTk` has:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, figure=None, master=None):\n        ...\n        self._tkcanvas = tk.Canvas(...)\n        ...\n    def draw(self):\n        ...\n```\nAnd there's `self.device_pixel_ratio`! In newer matplotlib, `FigureCanvasTkAgg` sets `self.figure.dpi = ...`? Actually `FigureCanvasAgg` does:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, figure=None):\n        super().__init__(figure=figure)\n        self._last_renderer = None\n        ...\n```\nand `_backend_gtk`/`_backend_tk` set `device_pixel_ratio` which multiplies the *renderer* dpi: `RendererAgg(width, height, self.figure.dpi * self.device_pixel_ratio)`? Something like: in `backend_agg.RendererAgg`, there's `self._dpi`. And `FigureCanvasAgg.get_renderer()` returns `self._last_renderer` created with `dpi = self.figure.dpi * self.device_pixel_ratio`? Hmm, if `device_pixel_ratio` were absurd... on a HiDPI X11 setup, could be 2, not 1e6.\n\nOK. New approach: what in this script could produce a glyph with a huge size? NONE. So maybe the raster overflow is not about size but about **a NaN/garbage rotation matrix**. Where does a rotation come from? `Text.set_rotation` — default 0. BUT `draw_text` in backend_agg:\n\n```python\n        font = self._prepare_font(prop)\n        font.set_size(prop.get_size_in_points(), self.dpi)  # maybe\n        font.set_text(s, angle)\n```\n\nHmm, in newer mpl (3.10/3.11), `draw_text` was refactored to:\n\n```python\n    def draw_text(self, gc, x, y, s, prop, angle, ismath=False, mtext=None):\n        # docstring inherited\n        if ismath:\n            return self.draw_mathtext(gc, x, y, s, prop, angle)\n\n        flags = get_hinting_flag()\n        font = self._prepare_font(prop)\n        font.set_text(s, angle, flags=flags)\n        font.draw_glyphs_to_bitmap(antialiased=...)\n```\nand `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes`:\n```python\n            for glyph, x, y in zip(font.get_glyphs...):\n                bitmap = font._render_glyph(glyph, flags)  # ?\n```\nThe summary in the traceback shows `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` at line 189 in backend_agg.py, and inside it `bitmap = font._render_glyph(...)`.\n\nLet me look at ft2font's `_render_glyph` doc/pyi: it likely takes `(gindex, hinting_factor, *, _kerning_factor)`.\n\nHonestly, the decisive step: search the web for the exact error + matplotlib. There are known GitHub issues. Let me do that.\n\nAlso worth checking: is this crash triggered by **a specific frame** (e.g., when the target's projected coordinate becomes ±huge, or when NaN)? If the crash is NaN-related, the fix is to sanitize: skip drawing when coordinates aren't finite.\n\nGiven the conversation: the script now (per my earlier suggestion the user may have applied? No — chat mode, I never edited; the user may have edited themselves) has `v`/`a` giving a 100 m trajectory with the image panel window centered at u≈3361 and *huge* limits. Then `Text` in the image axes at position (u[m].mean(), v[m].mean()) with u up to 7913 — that's a big data coordinate but transforms to a modest pixel position (the axes maps its limits to the axes box). Fine.\n\nHmm, but for the *world/armor* panels: `set_cube` with 100 m span... and `Text3D` projected coords can be ~1e4 in \"axes data\" space; `Text.draw` maps via `self.get_transform()`. For Text3D, if `_transform` is the IdentityTransform, then x,y are *pixels* → a coordinate of 1e4 → drawn off-canvas (clipped). Not a crash.\n\nHmm, BUT: if the Text3D's transform is NOT identity but something derived from the 3D projection (like `self.axes.transData`?) then a data coordinate of 1e4 maps to huge pixel coords... still not glyph size.\n\nUnless!!! `axes.draw_artist` with text: `Text.draw` → `_get_layout` → `renderer.get_text_width_height_descent(...)` → the *hinting* and *width* fine.\n\nI keep going in circles; the raster overflow must come from the FT size or matrix. Let me check `_prepare_font`: \n\n```python\n    def _prepare_font(self, font_prop):\n        font = _font_from_prop(font_prop)  # cached\n        font.clear()\n        font.set_size(font_prop.get_size_in_points(), self.dpi)\n        return font\n```\nWait — `self.dpi`! In `RendererAgg`, `self.dpi` is set in `__init__(self, width, height, dpi)`. And `figure.canvas.get_renderer()` creates the renderer with `self.figure.dpi`... \n\nBut here's the thing: **`FigureCanvasAgg.blit()` and blitting** — no.\n\nHmm, `self.dpi` could be huge if `figure.dpi` is huge. Is there anything in the script setting dpi? No. Default 100.\n\nSo glyph size ~ 8pt*100/72 ≈ 11 px. Definitely not overflow.\n\n=> Therefore the overflow must be due to a **degenerate transform matrix passed to FreeType**. In mpl 3.10+, `draw_text` builds a transform for the glyph positions; and there's `font.set_text(s, angle, flags)`, where `angle` and the *mtext* transform... In `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes`, mpl uses `mtext.get_transform()`?? Hmm, maybe: for the glyph bitmap, mpl calls `font._render_glyph(g, hinting_factor)` and separately computes `glyph.transform`? I don't recall.\n\nActually! I remember now: matplotlib 3.9 added `ft2font.FT2Image` and `_render_glyph` uses `FT_Set_Transform` with the angle matrix. If `angle` is NaN → `FT_Matrix` with NaNs → huge bbox → Raster_Overflow. In the traceback, `draw_text` line 245 → `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` line 189 → `font._render_glyph`. So I need to see backend_agg.py lines 170-250.\n\nLet me just read it. And search the web concurrently.\n\nI'll do:\n- read_file .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/matplotlib/backends/backend_agg.py offset 150 limit 120\n- web_search \"FT_Render_Glyph failed with error 0x62: raster overflow matplotlib\"\n\nNote the venv path has python3.11 fixed. Earlier I used a glob in shell. For read_file I need the actual path: `/home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/.venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/...` but read_file is workspace-relative → `.venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/matplotlib/backends/backend_agg.py`.\n\nLet me do it."}
