uge → **Raster_Overflow**! \n\nYes! That's a very plausible mechanism: with `set_box_aspect((1,1,1))` and a 100 m range, a point far outside the data box can land near the projection's eye plane, making the projected coordinates enormous (or negative w → mirrored). A `Text3D` at such a point → `set_position` → transform → delta astronomically large → `FT_Set_Transform` delta overflows/garbage → `FT_Render_Glyph` → Raster_Overflow.\n\nAlso `self.height - y` where y is 1e12 → `round(0x40*x)` fine (Python int) but `FT_Vector` delta = 6.4e13 → beyond the raster pool → overflow.\n\nThat's consistent and explains why it appeared *after* the user added motion, and why the first message (compressed summary era) also had it — maybe they had already tried motion then.\n\nNow: how to be sure? I can't run python. But I can check the plausibility via the `feet`: `float('nan')`? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively the crash could be from NaN positions: if `proj_transform` gives NaN (e.g., w = 0 exactly → division by zero → inf → inf*0 → nan). Then `round(0x40*nan)` → ValueError, not this. So it's finite-but-huge.\n\nHmm, one more candidate: `Text3D.draw` for a point where w < 0 (behind the eye) → projected coords are \"mirrored\" and can be huge. \n\nI'll present the mechanism as: 某个我们 blit 的 Text3D 的投影坐标爆炸（点在 3D 投影的\"眼平面\"附近，w→0，或者远在数据盒外）→ FreeType 的 delta 变成天文数字 → 光栅器 bbox 溢出。And I'll give the general diagnosis path + fixes:\n\n**Diagnosis (how to confirm)**: print the positions of the 12 texts just before drawing, or wrap `Text.draw` to log `self.get_position()` and `self.get_transform().transform(...)`. Since I can't run, give the user a snippet. Actually better: I can propose a small standalone repro script they run.\n\n**Fixes** (robust, minimal):\n1. **Don't put a Text3D where the projection can explode** — the root cause is `place()` putting labels at the *origin* of a frame that can be far outside the panel's data box. Fix: clamp/skip. Concretely, in `update()`, hide the label when the frame origin is outside the panel's limits:\n   ```python\n   inside = ((lo <= origin) & (origin <= hi)).all()\n   label.set_visible(inside)\n   ```\n   Since `label` is in `moving`, `set_visible(False)` means it's not drawn → no crash. Cheap and honest (a label outside the box is meaningless anyway).\n2. Alternatively set the label's position to a *clipped* position (clamp to the box) so it stays visible at the border.\n3. Or drop the triad labels from blit (bake into background) — but then they'd be wrong when moving. No.\n4. Or use a plain 2D text overlay in axes coordinates (annotations in a corner) instead of Text3D → immune to projection. For \"W/C/B/A#\" tags this is arguably better UX.\n5. Reduce the trajectory envelope (as discussed) so nothing leaves the box — the same fix as before.\n\nAlso mention: the crash is in the **blit path** (`draw_artist`) which is why it only shows up now; with a full redraw, the same Text3D would just be clipped by the axes' clip path... hmm, actually with a full redraw it would also be drawn (Text3D clips to the axes bbox? Text has `set_clip_on(True)` by default with `clip_box`?). In a full draw, the text is still rasterized (clipping happens after rasterization in Agg), so it would crash too. Hmm. So maybe not specific to blit.\n\nHmm, but is `_render_glyph` also called in a normal draw? Yes, `draw_text` → `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes`. So a full draw would crash identically. So it's not blit-specific. OK, so my earlier framing \"blit path\" is wrong — I should say it's the new mpl 3.11 per-glyph text path, used by both.\n\nHmm, wait. Then why did the crash appear at the *very start* of the session (first message) — the user was then running the version with... whatever. Both consistent with \"some Text3D position explodes\".\n\nLet me sanity check the \"w≈0\" theory numerically for the 100 m scene:\nWorld panel: cube center (7,-50,0.5), half 50.4. Axes3D default view: elevation 30°, azimuth -60°, `dist` = 10 (in axes units) — the eye is at 10× the box \"radius\" from the center along the view direction. Points are then projected with a perspective transform with `dist` ~10 (normalized box units, where the box half-size maps to 1). A point at the camera position (0,0,0.5): relative to the center = (-7, 50, 0) → in box units (-0.14, +0.99, 0). That's within the box. So no explosion. The body at (11,-100,0.5): relative (4,-50,0) → (0.08, -0.99, 0) — inside the box. So *no* explosion in the world panel! Hmm. Because both are on the box surface in normalized terms.\n\nArmor panel: cube from corners + BODY_POS = (3,0,0.5) → center ~((2.7+11.3)/2, (-100.3+0)/2) = (7, -50) with half 50.4 — same. So also fine: 'B' label at BODY_POS (3,0,0.5) → relative (-4, 50, 0)/50.4 = (-0.08, 0.99) — inside. Fine.\n\nHmm! So no explosion. So the theory fails for this scene.\n\nUnless the *label* is fine but the *image panel tags* are at u huge → the display coordinate ~7400 px. Would that overflow? Let me think about FreeType's raster more carefully: `FT_Set_Transform(face, matrix, delta)` then `FT_Load_Glyph` with `FT_LOAD_NO_BITMAP`? Then `FT_Render_Glyph`. The gray rasterizer (`ftgrays`) computes:\n\n```c\n    /* compute the bbox in 26.6 */\n    ...\n    ras.min_ex = (short)?? \n```\nHmm, in `ftgrays.c`:\n\n```c\n  static FT_Error\n  gray_convert_glyph_inner( RAS_ARG, ... )\n  ...\n  /* the band size */\n```\nand the *cbox* returned: `FT_Outline_Get_CBox` gives the bbox in 26.6 *font units*? No, in pixels*64.\n\nThen:\n```c\n    /* compute the width and height of the bitmap */\n    ...\n    if ( !( ras.min_ex < ras.max_ex && ras.min_ey < ras.max_ey ) ) ... empty\n    ras.width  = ( ras.max_ex - ras.min_ex + 32 ) >> 6;   /* pixels */\n```\nHmm, that's extent, not position. Then:\n```c\n    /* the render pool */\n    if ( ras.width > 0 && ( ras.height > FT_MAX_... ) )\n```\nHonestly, in FreeType's `ftraster.c` (the old raster1 used by default? No, default is ftsmooth/\"smooth\" rasterizer = ftgrays), there is this in `ftgrays.c`:\n\n```c\n      /* compute the cells bounding box */\n      ...\n      if ( ras.max_ex > ras.min_ex + 0x7FFF ) error = Raster_Overflow?\n```\n\nI recall a real matplotlib bug report: **\"Raster_Overflow when drawing text far outside the figure\"** — texts at large coordinates (like 1e6+) crash. Yes, I'm fairly confident: matplotlib issue #26018 or similar, \"FT_Render_Glyph failed with error 0x62: raster overflow\" when a tick label is placed at a huge position (e.g., log-scale plot with a limit of 1e300 → tick label at −1e18 px). YES — I remember that: **the error occurs with huge axis limits / degenerate transforms placing tick text far off-canvas**, common with `set_xlim(1e18)`. The fix in mpl was to not draw text whose position is `> SHRT_MAX`? Hmm.\n\nSo: huge *pixel* coordinate → Raster_Overflow. That's the documented behavior. So O(1e4) px shouldn't be enough; you need O(1e5)+ or more.\n\nLet me compute the image tag display x more carefully in the *current* 100 m scenario... 7421 px. Hmm.\n\nBut wait — what if the user ALSO applied my other suggestion? E.g., they set `ax_image` limits from the optical axis. Or they changed `set_cube`... Unknown.\n\nAlternatively maybe the crash is in the **3D panels for a different reason**: `Axes3D` sets `self.M` per draw. But with **blit + `draw_artist`**, `Text3D.draw` uses `self.axes.M` — which is fine... unless `Axes3D.draw()` hasn't run since the limits changed. Fixed limits → fine.\n\nHmm, hold on: what about `ax_world`'s *z-axis label*? Not in moving.\n\nLet me reconsider: are we sure the failing artist is in `moving`? `_blit_draw` → `for a in artists: a.axes.draw_artist(a)` — artists = `self._drawn_artists` = our list. So yes.\n\nHmm, unless `draw_artist` for a Text also triggers... no.\n\nOK so among 12 texts. Their positions come from `place()` (Text3D) and `set_position` (tags). So candidates: B label (body pos), A# labels (armor centers), tags (projected centers).\n\nNow: **what if the 100 m trajectory makes the image-panel tags' u/v astronomically large?** u = 640 + 800*(-y)/x where x = 3+0.8t ≥ 3 → no blowup. Max 7913.\n\nWhat about the **A# labels in the armor panel** at armor centers: (3+0.8t, -t²+ring) — inside the cube → fine.\n\nSo under the 100 m scenario, no astronomical position. So the crash must come from a *different* modification the user made — e.g., they may have set `DISTANCE` small, or added `rw_sigma` (random walk)! \n\n**rw_sigma!** The user just asked what rw_sigma is — maybe they *enabled* it. With rw_sigma nonzero in the armor/world panel, `set_cube` still uses `corners` computed from the same track → consistent. But the *cumsum* random walk can make positions huge over 600 steps (σ=0.1 → 2.4 m). Not astronomical.\n\nHmm, and: does `rw_sigma` get included in `set_cube`? Yes, corners come from the track which includes the walk. So consistent.\n\nOK, so what if the user, following my previous suggestion, centered the image window on the optical axis AND kept the 100 m trajectory? Then tags at u up to 7913 with window [493,787] → display x = 1187 + (7913-493)/294*390 ≈ 1187 + 9842 = 11029. Still 1.1e4.\n\nI need to know whether Raster_Overflow triggers at 1e4 px. Hmm. Let me look at the actual `_set_transform` implementation... it's compiled. But the `.pyi` may document units. And maybe `ft2font` is pure-python? No.\n\nHmm, what about the delta being *negative* huge: `self.height - y`; if y is huge positive → negative, fine.\n\nAlternatively the matrix: `(0x10000 * ... ).round().astype(int)` — for angle=0 it's `[[65536,0],[0,65536]]`. Note `extend` and `slant` are 1 and 0 in `draw_text`. Fine.\n\nSo, if not position, then **size**: `size = prop.get_size_in_points()`. What if a Text's fontsize is `np.inf` or a huge number? Where could that come from? `ax_image.set_title(f\"...\")` no.\n\nHmm, hold on: `self.dpi` in `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` — `font.set_size(size, self.dpi)`. `self` is the RendererAgg. Its `.dpi` attribute — for the renderer obtained from `canvas.get_renderer()`. In TkAgg, `get_renderer` uses `self.figure.dpi * self.device_pixel_ratio`? If `device_pixel_ratio` is garbage/huge (e.g., from `tk scaling`), then dpi huge → glyph huge → Raster_Overflow!\n\nLet me check `_backend_tk.py` for `device_pixel_ratio`. That's a concrete, checkable fact. If Tk sets `device_pixel_ratio = self._tkcanvas.winfo_fpixels('1i')/72` or similar, on a HiDPI screen it's ~2. Not huge.\n\nBut: `FigureCanvasTk` might set `self.device_pixel_ratio = self._tkcanvas.winfo_fpixels('1i') / 72` — that's DPI/72 ≈ 1.33. Then renderer dpi = 100*1.33 = 133 → glyph 15 px. Fine.\n\nLet me just grep for device_pixel_ratio in _backend_tk.py and figure.py. Also grep for `get_renderer` in backend_agg.\n\nLet me do a few greps. Then decide. Also maybe grep the mpl CHANGELOG? No.\n\nActually — wait. A much simpler hypothesis I overlooked: **`ax.text(0, 0, f\"A{m}\", fontsize=8)` in the image panel is placed at *data* coords, but at frame 0 the tags are at (u.mean, v.mean) which is fine... BUT `_blit_clear`/`restore_region` doesn't matter.\n\nHmm, let me look at this from the \"what's new in mpl 3.11\" angle: the new text path `_layout` + `_set_transform` + `_render_glyph` was introduced in 3.10 (the \"text layout\" rework). There were bug reports: \"raster overflow\" when drawing text with **`usetex=False` and a large dpi**... \n\nHmm, notably: `font._set_transform(matrix, delta)` sets the *face* transform to include the *position*. That's new (3.10+). And there IS a known matplotlib bug: **blitting text at positions far outside the canvas raises `RuntimeError: FT_Render_Glyph ... raster overflow`** because the delta is passed to FreeType, and FreeType's rasterizer computes the bbox from `outline + delta`, and when |delta| > ~2^? the internal 16.16/26.6 math overflows → Raster_Overflow. In the old path, the delta was applied by Agg *after* rasterization, so off-canvas text was harmless. **This is a regression in the new text path.** I'm now fairly confident: the delta in 26.6 units is `0x40 * x`; if x is ~1e4 px → 6.4e5, fine for 32-bit. But FreeType's gray rasterizer converts to `TCoord` and computes `(x + 32) >> 6` → 1e4. The raster pool/spans then use `ras.max_ex` etc. There's a check in ftgrays:\n\n```c\n    if ( ras.max_ex - ras.min_ex > 0x7FFF || ... )  /* too large */\n```\nNo, the extent is 11 px.\n\nHmm hmm. But in `ftgrays.c` the *cells* are computed per scanline band and use the *absolute* y: `ras.min_ey`, `ras.max_ey`; and there's:\n\n```c\n      /* the cells must fit in the buffer */\n      if ( FT_NEW_ARRAY( ras.cells, n ) ) ...\n```\nn = width * height / ... small.\n\nI recall in `ftgrays.c`:\n```c\n    /* Compute the width and height of the bitmap */\n    ...\n    if ( cbox.xMin < 0 || cbox.yMin < 0 || cbox.xMax > ... )\n      error = FT_Err_Raster_Overflow;\n```\nHmm.\n\nActually! In `ftraster.c` (used when FT_CONFIG_OPTION_SUBPIXEL_RENDERING off? no)... \n\nLet me look for the actual code: FreeType's `ftgrays.c` function `gray_convert_glyph_inner` + `gray_convert_glyph`, and `FT_Render_Glyph_Internal` in `ftobjs.c`:\n\n```c\n    if ( slot->format == FT_GLYPH_FORMAT_OUTLINE ) {\n      ...\n      if ( width > 0 && height > 0 ) ...\n```\nand in `ftobjs.c` there's:\n\n```c\n    /* check that the bitmap dimensions are not too large */\n    if ( slot->bitmap.width > ... )\n```\n\nHonestly: known mpl issues about \"raster overflow\" mention **huge tick label offsets** (e.g., with `log` axes and `1e300` limits, positions like 1e20 px). So the threshold is likely in the 1e5–1e9 px range, not 1e4.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, the best answer: explain the mechanism (FreeType rasterizes the glyph at an absolute pixel offset; an absurd offset or size makes the raster bbox overflow) and give a **diagnostic procedure** the user can run to find which artist, plus robust fixes that don't depend on knowing the exact threshold:\n\nDiagnostic (one-liner in the script, temporarily):\n```python\ndef _probe(a):\n    p = a.get_position()\n    d = a.get_transform().transform(p)\n    print(type(a).__name__, getattr(a, \"get_text\", lambda: \"\")(), p, d)\nfor a in moving: _probe(a)\n```\nIf any display coordinate has |x| or |y| > 1e4 (or is inf/nan), that's the culprit.\n\nRobust fixes:\n1. **Clamp/hide off-panel labels**: in `place()`, hide the label if the origin is outside the axes limits (and for tags, hide if outside the window). Both are cheap `set_visible()` calls on moving artists.\n2. **Don't let the projected tag go off-scale**: clamp the tag position into the axes range:\n   ```python\n   ax = ax_image\n   x0, x1 = sorted(ax.get_xlim()); y0, y1 = sorted(ax.get_ylim())\n   tags[m].set_position((np.clip(u[m].mean(), x0, x1), np.clip(v[m].mean(), y0, y1)))\n   ```\n   This keeps the tag at the border (nice UX: you see where it went) and guarantees a sane pixel offset.\n3. **Use 2D annotations in axes coords** for the corner labels instead of Text3D — immune to projection issues.\n4. Reduce the envelope (the earlier design fix).\n5. As a general guard: `if not np.isfinite(...) or abs(...) > 1e5: set_visible(False)`.\n\nAlso mention: this is a **matplotlib 3.10+ text-path regression** (the new per-glyph `_set_transform` passes the absolute position into FreeType; older mpl applied the offset in Agg after rasterization, so off-canvas text was harmless) — that's why the traceback goes through `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes`, a function that didn't exist before 3.10. I should hedge: \"从 3.10 起的新文本路径\" — I'm fairly confident `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` is new (3.10/3.11). The user has mpl 3.11.2. I can verify by checking whether the function exists in the installed version and its docstring/comments. It's there (we read it). Whether it's new in 3.10 — I believe so; there's a changelog note \"text rendering now uses _layout\"/\"FT2Font._layout\". Hmm, `font._layout` and `item.ft_object`/`glyphs` are from the 3.10 \"text layout refactor\" by QuLogic. I'm reasonably confident. I'll phrase it carefully: \"3.10 引入的新文本路径（`_layout`/`_set_transform`/`_render_glyph`）\" with a hedge \"如果你回退到 3.9 会看到这条路径不存在\".\n\nHmm, careful — the summary said earlier the user could downgrade? No. Just mention.\n\nAlso, why does the height calc use `self.height - y`: for the image panel tags with y huge, `self.height - y` huge negative.\n\nNow the most useful part: **which artist and why**. I should give the *most likely* answer given the current script state (the user's edited version with v/a + maybe more). Let me reason about which of the 12 texts can have an absurd position **in the current code**:\n\n- tags: at (u.mean, v.mean). With v/a: u up to 7913, and the window center = mean 3361 → |x - center| up to 4552 → display x ≈ 1187 + (4552/147)*195 ≈ 7200 px. Hmm: the axes is ~390 px wide, so the scale is 390/294 = 1.33 per data px → Δ = 4552*1.33 = 6044 px → display x ≈ 1187 + 6044 = 7231. So ~7e3 px. If FreeType's threshold is much higher, no crash. Hmm.\n\n- What if the user *also* enabled `rw_sigma`? Then `corners` include the cumulative walk... still ~metres.\n\n- What if the user changed `DISTANCE`/`SECONDS` such that x approaches 0? e.g. v = -1 (moving toward the camera) → at t=3, x=0 → u = ±inf → **NaN/inf**! Then `u.mean()` = inf/nan → `set_position((inf, ...))` → `round(0x40*inf)` → OverflowError (not raster overflow) or nan → ValueError. Hmm. But if u is finite-but-huge like 1e8 (x = 3e-6 m), then display x = 1e8*1.33 → 1.3e8 → delta 8e9 → int32 overflow inside FreeType → garbage → **Raster_Overflow**! That fits *perfectly*: a target flying *toward/through* the camera plane produces astronomically large projections.\n\nIs that plausible? The user was told \"a=(-2) → parabola\"; maybe they also set negative v... Unknown. But the general fix is the same.\n\nI'll present it as: **最可能的触发点是\"某个我们 blit 的 Text 被投影到了天文数字级的像素坐标\"**（典型来源：目标在相机平面附近 x→0 使 u∝1/x 爆炸；或 3D 点跑到投影眼平面附近 w→0），并给出确认方法。\n\nThen the fixes. Also offer a defensive one-liner that will *guarantee* no recurrence:\n\n```python\ndef safe_text(txt, xy):\n    \"\"\"hide the label instead of putting it at an absurd pixel position\"\"\"\n    x, y = xy\n    if not np.isfinite(xy).all() or abs(x) > 1e5 or abs(y) > 1e5:\n        txt.set_visible(False)\n    else:\n        txt.set_visible(True)\n        txt.set_position(xy)\n```\n\nAnd for 3D labels: check `np.isfinite(origin).all()` and whether it's inside the axes limits.\n\nSince chat mode can't edit, offer to do it in work mode.\n\nAlso worth noting: it's *not* caused by `blit` per se — a full redraw hits the same `_render_glyph`. But blit makes it *deterministic per frame*, i.e., you now see it at the frame where the bad position occurs.\n\nHmm, actually one more consideration on *which* text: with `draw_artist`, the text is drawn **even if outside the axes clip box**? In a full draw, Agg still rasterizes glyphs regardless of clipping. So same.\n\nLet me also double check `Text.draw` line ~909 to confirm there's no clamping. Let me read text.py around 880-915 to be accurate about `angle` and the position path. It's cheap and increases precision. Also grep `_backend_tk.py` for device_pixel_ratio to close the dpi question.\n\nLet me do those two reads/greps."}
