{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793180359.5405385, "id": "f9ee4bf25411", "tool_call_id": "call_00_xJCD4LSrRtpwGpsblqy65163", "content": "250:                 root = weakroot()\n251:                 if root is not None:\n252:                     root.unbind(\"<Destroy>\", filter_destroy_id)\n253:                 return\n254:             if event.widget is self._tkcanvas:\n255:                 CloseEvent(\"close_event\", self)._process()\n256:         filter_destroy_id = root.bind(\"<Destroy>\", filter_destroy, \"+\")\n257: \n258:         self._tkcanvas.focus_set()\n259: \n260:         self._rubberband_rect_black = None\n261:         self._rubberband_rect_white = None\n262: \n263:     def _update_device_pixel_ratio(self, event=None):\n264:         ratio = None\n265:         if sys.platform == 'win32':\n266:             # Tk gives scaling with respect to 72 DPI, but Windows screens are\n267:             # scaled vs 96 dpi, and pixel ratio settings are given in whole\n268:             # percentages, so round to 2 digits.\n269:             ratio = round(self._tkcanvas.tk.call('tk', 'scaling') / (96 / 72), 2)\n270:         elif sys.platform == \"linux\":\n271:             ratio = self._tkcanvas.winfo_fpixels('1i') / 96\n272:         if ratio is not None and self._set_device_pixel_ratio(ratio):\n273:             # Resize the canvas widget, then explicitly update the figure\n274:             # size to match the actual widget dimensions.  When the canvas\n275:             # is constrained by a geometry manager (pack/grid), <Configure>\n276:             # may not fire after configure(), so we handle the resize\n277:             # directly — similar to Qt's _update_pixel_ratio approach.\n278:             w, h = self.get_width_height(physical=True)\n279:             self._tkcanvas.configure(width=w, height=h)\n280:             self._resize_figure_for_canvas_size(\n281:                 self._tkcanvas.winfo_width(),\n282:                 self._tkcanvas.winfo_height())\n283: \n284:     def resize(self, event):\n285:         self._resize_figure_for_canvas_size(event.width, event.height)\n286: \n287:     def _resize_figure_for_canvas_size(self, width, height):\n288:         \"\"\"Update figure size and redraw based on a given canvas size.\"\"\"\n289:         if width <= 0 or height <= 0:\n... (showing lines 250-289 of 1125; use offset=290 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1793180359.5523756, "id": "bc761cd9c94d", "tool_call_id": "call_01_0DFtnoAyMNpOJqh6UrT07499", "content": "OK: command succeeded\nstdout:\n443:    def get_renderer(self):\n444-        w, h = self.get_width_height(physical=True)\n445-        key = w, h, self.figure.dpi\n446-        reuse_renderer = (self._lastKey == key)\n447-        if not reuse_renderer:\n448-            self.renderer = RendererAgg(w, h, self.figure.dpi)\n449-            self._lastKey = key\n450-        return self.renderer\n451-\n452-    def tostring_argb(self):\n453-        \"\"\"\n454-        Get the image as ARGB `bytes`.\n455-\n456-        `draw` must be called at least once before this function will work and\n457-        to update the renderer for any subsequent changes to the Figure.\n458-        \"\"\"\n459-        return self.renderer.tostring_argb()\n460-\n461-    def buffer_rgba(self):\n462-        \"\"\"\n463-        Get the image as a `memoryview` to the renderer's buffer.\n464-\n465-        `draw` must be called at least once before this function will work and\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1793180385.6569343, "id": "9968a4d88a30", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_oXWv6zKRkWJgLZxxB0Ei5890", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation/.venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/matplotlib && ls ft2font*.so && strings -a ft2font*.so | grep -i -m20 \\\"raster\\\\|negative height\\\\|render_glyph\\\"\"}"}, {"id": "call_01_Pq4hdBlyM9Z6qN5JVvAY4833", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /home/fanshu/Workspace/qianli_auto_aim_simulation && grep -rn \\\"Raster_Overflow\\\\|0x62\\\\|negative height\\\" .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/matplotlib/*.py .venv/lib/python3.11/site-packages/matplotlib/**/*.pyi 2>/dev/null | head\"}"}], "reasoning": "`get_renderer` uses `self.figure.dpi` (not multiplied by device_pixel_ratio — but `get_width_height(physical=True)` multiplies by dpi_ratio). So the renderer's dpi = figure.dpi = 100. Glyph size for 8pt = 11 px. So `size`/dpi is not the problem (unless figure.dpi is huge, which it isn't).\n\nAlso note: `RendererAgg(w, h, self.figure.dpi)` — h = physical height. So `self.height` in `_draw_text_glyphs_and_boxes` = h (physical, ~520*ratio).\n\nInteresting: `_update_device_pixel_ratio` on Linux: `ratio = winfo_fpixels('1i')/96`. On the user's machine that's probably 1.0 (or 1.33). If the ratio were e.g. 2, the renderer is 3300x1040 — fine.\n\nSo the remaining candidate is the *position* (delta). Confirmed by code: `delta = [round(0x40*(x + dx*cos - dy*sin)), round(0x40*(self.height - y + ...))]` where x,y are display pixels.\n\nSo: a text at a huge display coordinate → FT delta huge → raster overflow. And the size is definitely small, so the overflow must come from the huge delta. That is consistent with FreeType's rasterizer: it computes the glyph bbox including the translation, and `FT_Outline_Get_CBox` gives coordinates like 6.4e5*... and the gray rasterizer's `ras.max_ex/min_ex` (in 26.6) are then `int` truncated in places... In `ftgrays.c`:\n\n```c\n      if ( ras.max_ex > ras.min_ex + 0x7FFF ) ...\n```\nHmm, actually I recall in `ftgrays.c`:\n```c\n    /* compute the width and height of the bitmap */\n    ...\n    if ( !( width > 0 && height > 0 ) ) ...\n```\nand in `ftobjs.c` `FT_Render_Glyph_Internal`:\n```c\n      if ( error ) goto Exit;\n```\nThere IS a check in ftobjs.c:\n```c\n    /* if the glyph's bbox is too large, return Raster_Overflow? */\n```\nAnd in `ftgrays.c` `gray_convert_glyph_inner(...)`:\n```c\n    if ( ras.max_ey - ras.min_ey > ... )\n```\n\nI'll present the mechanism at the level of \"FreeType 会在光栅器里按这个 26.6 绝对偏移铺 span；偏移超出光栅器能表示的范围（它内部用 16.16/26.6 定点的 int）就会返回 Raster_Overflow (0x62)\". That's accurate enough: the delta is 26.6 and FreeType's internal coordinate for the raster is `int`-ish; values ≳ 2^? become garbage.\n\nActually — important: FreeType's `FT_Pos` is `long` (64-bit), but the *rasterizer* (`ftgrays.c`) uses `TCoord` = `signed long`? and then `FT_Int` casts. There's `ras.min_ex`, `ras.max_ex` as `long`. Hmm.\n\nWhatever. The empirical, well-known symptom: text at very large coordinates (like 1e6 px) → this exact error. In matplotlib's tracker there are issues with tick labels at extreme coordinates. So I'll assert: 触发条件是文本的**显示坐标**大到离谱（大致 ≳1e5 px 量级），或字体尺寸离谱；这里字体是 8pt，所以是前者。\n\nNow, is there a plausible huge display coordinate in the current script? I need to give the user a definitive answer. Let me think about which text can have a huge position *in the current script state* (with whatever they hacked in). I can't see their edits. So I should:\n1. Explain the mechanism (precise, with code citations).\n2. Say: 这个异常一定能定位到 `moving` 里的某个 Text（只有这 12 个文本会走 `draw_artist`），给出探针代码，打印它们的显示坐标，异常的那个会露出来。\n3. Give the likely candidates in this specific script:\n   - `tags[m]`（图像面板的 A0..A3）：位置 = 投影中心。目标接近相机平面（x→0，比如给了负的 v 或大的 |a| 让它朝相机/穿过相机）时 u ∝ 1/x 爆炸 → 显示坐标 1e6 级。\n   - `Text3D` 三轴标签（W/C/B/A#）：`Text3D.draw` 用 `axes.M` 投影后 `set_position`；若点落到投影的\"眼平面\"附近（w→0）或远在数据盒外，坐标爆炸。\n   - 如果 `set_xlim` 拿到 NaN/inf（比如 u_all 里出现 inf），整块 axes 的 transform 变成 1e300 级 → 任何文本都爆。\n4. Fixes: clamp/hide, guard `np.isfinite`, and the design-level fix (bounded trajectory + fixed arena limits + optical-axis-centered camera window) — i.e., 回到我们前面讨论的\"有界包络\"。\n\nAlso mention: it's **not** a blit bug; the same code path (`_render_glyph`) runs in a full redraw, so disabling blit won't help. But: with blit you can see *which frame* it dies on. Hmm, actually a useful test: 关掉 blit 看是否还崩（应该还崩，且崩在同一帧），这能证明与 blit 无关。\n\nAlso: a quick sanity check the user can run: `print(np.isfinite(u_all).all(), np.abs(u_all).max())` etc.\n\nAnd: `float` display coords probe:\n\n```python\nfor a in moving:\n    if isinstance(a, mpl.text.Text):\n        px, py = a.get_transform().transform(a.get_position())\n        print(f\"{a.get_text()!r:6} pos={a.get_position()} px=({px:.1f},{py:.1f})\")\n```\n\nNote for Text3D the position is set during draw, so probe inside `update()` right after `place()` — the position then is data coords; the transform for Text3D is... let me think: `Axes.text` → `Text` with `transform=self.transData`? In `_axes.py`:\n\n```python\n    def text(self, x, y, s, fontdict=None, **kwargs):\n        ...\n        t = mtext.Text(x, y, text=s, **effective_kwargs)\n        ...\n        self._add_text(t)\n        return t\n```\nand `Text.__init__` sets `self._transform = kwargs.pop('transform', mtransforms.IdentityTransform())`! Yes — Text's default transform is IdentityTransform. So for a plain Text in the axes, `Axes.text` passes no transform → IdentityTransform → then `Axes._add_text` does `t.set_transform(self.transData)`? Hmm, I recall `Axes.text` does:\n\n```python\n        t = mtext.Text(x, y, text=s, **effective_kwargs)\n        t.set_clip_path(self.patch)\n        t.set_figure(self.figure)\n        self._add_text(t)\n```\nand `_add_text` appends. Then `draw` uses `t.get_transform()` = IdentityTransform?? No — `Annotation` differs. Hmm, for `Axes.text`, the transform must be transData. Let me check: `Text.__init__`: `self._transform = kwargs.pop('transform', mtransforms.IdentityTransform())`. And `Axes.text` passes `transform` in effective_kwargs? In `Axes.text`:\n\n```python\n        effective_kwargs = {\n            'verticalalignment': 'baseline',\n            'horizontalalignment': 'left',\n            'transform': self.transData,\n            'clip_on': False,\n            **fontdict,\n            **kwargs,\n        }\n```\nYes! `'transform': self.transData` is in the defaults. Good — so Text in an axes uses transData.\n\nFor `Axes3D.text`, it calls `super().text(x, y, s, **kwargs)`? Then transform = `self.transData` (the 2D transData of the 3D axes). And the projected 3D coords are in *2D data* units → transData maps them. Good.\n\nSo in `update`, right after `place()`, the Text3D's position is the *3D* origin (data units, e.g. (3,0,0.5)) — but `Text3D.draw` overwrites it with the projected value before drawing. So probing inside update won't reveal the crash position; the probe must be inside `Text3D.draw`. Simpler: probe the *display* coords by calling `proj3d.proj_transform(origin, ax.M)` manually after a draw. Or monkeypatch:\n\n```python\nfrom mpl_toolkits.mplot3d import proj3d\ndef probe(a):\n    if isinstance(a, mpl.text.Text):\n        p = a.get_position()\n        px, py = a.get_transform().transform(p)\n        print(type(a).__name__, a.get_text(), p, px, py)\n```\nand call it at the top of `traceback`... eh.\n\nBetter practical advice: monkeypatch `matplotlib.text.Text.draw` to log the display position, then re-run:\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.text as mtext\n_draw = mtext.Text.draw\ndef draw(self, renderer):\n    px, py = self.get_transform().transform(self.get_position())\n    if not (-1e4 < px < 1e4 and -1e4 < py < 1e4):\n        print(\"SUSPECT\", repr(self.get_text()), px, py)\n    return _draw(self, renderer)\nmtext.Text.draw = draw\n```\nThat prints the culprit — for Text3D, `draw` is overridden so `Text3D.draw` calls `super().draw(renderer)` → our wrapper sees the *projected* position. \n\nThen the fix: hide/clamp.\n\nGiven the conversational context (the user is iterating on the visualization, and we've already agreed on periodic bounded motion + fixed arena), the best answer ties this crash to that design: **它是\"目标跑得太远\"的第三种表现**（先变小黑点，再投影爆炸）。And the fix is the same: 有界的轨迹 + 固定的场地/视野 + 对越界标签做隐藏/clamp。\n\nLet me also answer \"这是怎么回事\" precisely at the top: FT error 0x62 = Raster_Overflow; the new mpl text path passes the absolute pixel offset into FreeType; glyph size is fine (8pt→11px), so the offset must be absurd.\n\nAlso mention the free-type error code table: 0x62 is `FT_Err_Raster_Overflow` — \"the rasterizer ran out of raster pool / outline too large\". Let me double check the numeric: FT_Err_Base = 0x00 ... error codes: 0x62 = 98 = FT_Err_Raster_Overflow. In ft2build/freetype/fterrdef.h: \n\n- 0x60 = Raster_Uninitialized (96)\n- 0x61 = Raster_Overflow (97)\n- 0x62 = Raster_Negative_Height (98)\n\nHmm! Let me recall the actual list:\n\n```c\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Raster_Uninitialized,                     0x60, \\\n                \"invalid raster object\" )\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Raster_Overflow,                          0x61, \\\n                \"raster overflow\" )\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Raster_Negative_Height,                   0x62, \\\n                \"negative height while rastering\" )\n```\n\nHmm! That's important. If 0x62 = Raster_Negative_Height, the message \"negative height while rastering\" would be printed, not \"raster overflow\". But matplotlib prints \"failed with error 0x62: raster overflow\". mpl's ft2font_wrapper has its own error string table! Let me check: the message in the traceback is \"FT_Render_Glyph (ft2font_wrapper.cpp line 1947) failed with error 0x62: raster overflow\". So mpl's fmt used FreeType's `FT_Error_String`? If it uses `FT_Error_String(error)`, the string for 0x62 is \"negative height while rastering\". Since it printed \"raster overflow\", maybe mpl's own table maps 0x62 → raster overflow. Hmm, or FreeType's `FT_Error_String` gives \"raster overflow\" for 0x62?\n\nLet me check the real fterrdef.h contents (from memory, FreeType 2.13):\n\n```c\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Raster_Uninitialized,                     0x60, \\\n                \"invalid raster object\" )\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Raster_Overflow,                          0x61, \\\n                \"raster overflow\" )\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Raster_Negative_Height,                   0x62, \\\n                \"negative height while rastering\" )\n```\n\nHmm, but there's also:\n```c\n  FT_ERRORDEF_( Invalid_Outline,                          0x14, ...)\n```\nAnd 0x62... Let me verify against matplotlib's ft2font_wrapper.cpp — is the source distributed in the wheel? Usually `.pyi` only. But maybe I can grep the site-packages for \"raster overflow\" string in the compiled .so? `grep -a \"raster overflow\" *.so` might reveal that mpl has its own table. Actually mpl's `ft2font_wrapper.cpp` has:\n\n```c\n    if (error) {\n      throw std::runtime_error(\n        \"FT_Render_Glyph (ft2font_wrapper.cpp line \" ... \" failed with error \" + ...);\n```\nHmm, the message format \"failed with error 0x62: raster overflow\" suggests it formats the error code and a string from a table. Let me grep the .so for \"raster overflow\" — if present, mpl has its own string table; then 0x62 → raster overflow in mpl's table.\n\nLet me do that: `grep -a -o \"raster overflow\" .venv/.../ft2font*.so | head` and also `strings | grep -i raster`.\n\nThis matters for the diagnosis: 0x62 could be \"negative height while rastering\" — which happens when the *bbox height* is negative, i.e., `yMin > yMax` — a degenerate/flipped outline, which is exactly what a garbage (overflowed) transform produces. Both interpretations point to the same cause: a degenerate transform from an absurd offset. Good: either way, the conclusion holds. But let me check to be precise.\n\nLet me grep the .so.\n\nAlso, let me check whether mpl's `_render_glyph` clamps anything.\n\nThen write the answer. Since the answer's core is \"absurd absolute pixel offset from off-panel text\", plus fixes, that's solid.\n\nLet me also compute what the *threshold* is. If FreeType's raster1/ftgrays uses 26.6 with `int` truncation to 16.16 (`FT_Pos` >> 6 → int), then |delta/64| > 2^31/64? Actually if the code does `(FT_Int)(x >> 6)` then values > 2^31/64 ≈ 3.3e7 px overflow. Hmm, that's 3e7 px — huge. But we might have that if the projections explode (inf-ish → but inf would be caught by round → OverflowError).\n\nHmm, so for a *finite* but huge value like 1e12 px, `round(0x40*1e12)` = 6.4e13 (FT_Pos is 64-bit, fine), then `>>6` → 1e12 → cast to int32 → garbage → negative bbox → \"negative height while rastering\" (0x62!). That fits *perfectly* with 0x62 = Raster_Negative_Height!\n\nSo: 0x62 in FreeType is likely `FT_Err_Raster_Negative_Height`, and mpl's message says \"raster overflow\" because... hmm, the traceback explicitly says \"raster overflow\". Maybe mpl's `_get_error_string` table maps 0x62 to \"raster overflow\" incorrectly, or FreeType's string is indeed \"negative height while rastering\" and mpl truncated/paraphrased. The traceback shows \"failed with error 0x62: raster overflow\", so mpl's table says raster overflow for 0x62. Let me check if mpl bundles a table. grep the .so.\n\nLet me run: `strings -a ft2font*.so | grep -i -m5 \"raster\"` and also grep for \"negative height\".\n\nThat will settle whether it's a coordinate overflow (negative height) or a genuine raster overflow. Either way the fix is the same, but the precision is valuable.\n\nAlso, since it's likely an int32 truncation of the delta, the threshold is |pixel| ≳ 3.3e7. That requires truly astronomic positions — consistent with a 1/x explosion where x is tiny (e.g., the target near the camera plane: x = 1e-6 → u = 8e8 → display 1e9 px → overflow!).\n\nSo: the trigger is likely the target crossing/nearing the camera plane, i.e., `cam_z = x ≈ 0` → `u = FX*cam_x/cam_z` → astronomic. In `update`, `u, v = project(plate)` with plate at the body position; if the body's x approaches 0 (target passes the camera), u explodes. With `v` negative (moving toward camera) or `a` pushing it, that happens!\n\nAlso `set_xlim(u_all.mean() ± half)` would then be garbage → all texts explode.\n\nSo the most likely user action: they added motion that brings the target to/through the camera (e.g. `v=(-1,0,0)` or a large negative a). Their earlier experiment had `a=-2` in y (lateral) — stays at x≥3. Hmm, but they might have added `v=(-0.8,...)`? Unknown. Or they added `rw_sigma` big? Hmm.\n\nI'll present: 「最可能是某个投影变成天文数字」并给出定位探针，同时列出三个具体嫌疑（目标穿过相机平面 x→0；3D 点靠近投影眼平面；窗口/limits 变成 NaN 或 inf）。\n\nAnd the fix set. Good.\n\nOne more thing to verify quickly: does `project()` guard cam_z? No. So x→0 → inf. `np.isfinite` guards absent.\n\nLet me run the strings grep, then write."}
