y're asking because they're confused about what I meant.\n\nWait, maybe the user hasn't run it yet, or maybe they changed it because of my claim. Also line 154 still uses BODY_POS for set_cube (fine).\n\nSo my answer: `body_pos` was a name I invented in my suggestion — it does not exist in the script; I meant a new variable `body_pos = centers.mean(1)`. And `centers[index]` is **not** it: that's 4 points (one per armor), shape (4,3). `place()` needs one point (3,). So the current state of lines 182-183 is wrong and will raise an error (or produce garbage).\n\nLet me verify what exactly would happen at runtime with centers[index] (4,3):\n- `place(frame, origin, rot)`: `for line, end in zip(lines, origin + length * rot.T)`. `origin` (4,3) + `rot.T` (3,3)*length → broadcast (4,3,3). zip(lines, ...) gives each `end` as (4,3) — the i-th axis direction for all four plates! Then `line.set_data_3d(*np.vstack([origin, end]).T)` → np.vstack([(4,3),(4,3)]) → (8,3) → .T → (3,8) → set_data_3d with 8 points each. So it would NOT error at that line! It would draw 8 points: the 4 plate centers followed by 4 offset points. Hmm, wait: `origin` is (4,3), `end` is (4,3) → vstack → (8,3) → .T (3,8) → set_data_3d(*(3,8)) → passes 8 x's, 8 y's, 8 z's. So a line with 8 vertices — a zigzag! No error.\n- `label.set_position_3d(origin)` with origin (4,3) → Text3D.set_position_3d(xs, ys, zs) signature: `set_position_3d(self, xyz, *, z=None)`. Let's check: Text3D.set_position_3d takes (xyz, z=None) and does `self._x, self._y, self._z = np.broadcast_arrays(*xyz, z)`? In mpl, Text3D.set_position_3d(xyz, z=None) — xyz can be a 3-tuple. If given a (4,3) array, then xyz = the whole array, z=None → likely `_x, _y, _z = np.broadcast_arrays(xyz, z)`... Let me not over-claim. Actually mpl text3d:\n\n```python\ndef set_position_3d(self, xyz, z=None):\n    if z is not None:\n        xyz = (xyz, z)\n    self._x, self._y, self._z = np.broadcast_arrays(*xyz)\n```\nHmm, if xyz is an (4,3) array, `*xyz` unpack